tel 1.0

base octogenarian
import soundness

info   Git as typed operations on a repository opened for a scope, with a backend that runs `git` or one that needs no `git` at all
detail
    _Octogenarian_ drives [Git](https://git-scm.com/) from Scala as typed operations on a
    repository handle, rather than as command strings assembled by hand and text output parsed
    afterwards. A branch (`Git.Branch`), a tag (`Git.Tag`) and a commit hash (`Git.Hash`) are
    distinct types, so an operation which expects a branch does not accept a tag; results come
    back as values (`Commit`, `FileDiff`, `Git.Head`, `Git.Merge.Outcome`) rather than as text to
    re-parse; failures are a `Git.Error` with a numbered `Git.Error.Reason`; and the API is honest
    about the one thing every Git library must be honest about: the repository is on disk, and
    other processes write it.

    That honesty has two halves. Every read which resolves a ref, HEAD, the index, the working
    files, the reflog, the stash, configuration, remotes or linked worktrees returns a `Stale`
    value: what it holds was true at the moment of the read, and may have changed since. And
    every write which moves a ref takes the `Stale` reading it is conditional on, and fails with
    `Git.Error(Moved(ref, expected, actual))` if the ref no longer holds it — Git's own
    compare-and-swap, `git update-ref ref new old`, made unavoidable, so that a commit cannot
    overwrite work another process committed between the read and the write.

    A repository is created with `Git.init` and opened, for the duration of a block, in one of
    two forms. `Git.Repo` is the object database and its refs, which a bare repository has;
    `Git.Worktree` is a checked-out tree, with an index and working files, over its repository.
    The mode names the grants the handle carries: a handle opened with `Read` alone cannot be
    used to write, which is a compile-time error rather than a runtime one.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
    import gitBackends.commandlineGit
    import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    val directory = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "git" / Uuid().show
    directory.create[Directory]()
    Git.init(directory, initialBranch = Git.Branch(t"main"))

    directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
      val head: Stale[Git.Head] = worktree.head()
      (worktree / "notes.txt").overwrite(t"Remember the milk")
      worktree.add(worktree / "notes.txt")
      worktree.commit(t"Add notes", head)
      worktree.log().value.map(_.message)   // List(t"Add notes")
    ```

    A `Git.Worktree.Handle` is also a `Git.Repo.Handle`, so every repository operation is
    available on it, and it is a galilei directory handle: paths formed from it, such as
    `worktree / "notes.txt"`, belong to this handle alone, cannot express `..`, and may be read
    and written with the filesystem operations under the same grants.

    Underneath, the operations are carried out by a **backend**, a `Git.Backend`, chosen by
    importing one given by name from the `gitBackends` package:

    - `gitBackends.commandlineGit` runs the `git` command found by
      `gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand`, in the `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in scope,
      logging each invocation as a `Commandline.Event`. It is the choice when git's own
      behaviour is wanted in full: hooks, filters, credential helpers, submodules.
    - `gitBackends.soundnessGit` is `Native()`, the backend which reads and writes the
      repository's files itself, in git's own formats, so a repository is always one `git`
      itself understands and an operation one side stops on a conflict the other can continue.
      It needs no `git` binary, so it runs wherever the JVM or Scala Native does. Objects
      (loose and packed), refs, the index, configuration, notes, linked worktrees, merging,
      rebasing and the stash are all its own.

    Which backend is in scope changes nothing about the code which uses it:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label Under the command-line backend
      code
          locally:
            import gitBackends.commandlineGit
            import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand

            val clone = directory / "clone-cli"
            Git.clone(directory, clone)

            clone.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
              worktree.switch(Git.Branch(t"feature"), worktree.head(), create = true)
              worktree.branches().value.keys   // Set(Branch(main), Branch(feature))
    frame
      label Under the native backend, which needs no `git`
      code
          locally:
            import gitBackends.soundnessGit

            val clone = directory / "clone-native"
            Git.clone(directory, clone)

            clone.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
              worktree.switch(Git.Branch(t"feature"), worktree.head(), create = true)
              worktree.branches().value.keys   // Set(Branch(main), Branch(feature))
    ##
    ```

    The native backend reaches a repository on the same filesystem by its path. A remote one
    needs a **transport**, and the `gitTransports` package has one given for each of the ways
    git reaches a server: `smartHttp` (git's smart protocol over whichever `Http.Backend` is in
    scope), `sshCommand` (a spawned `ssh`) and `soundnessSsh` (the native SSH client, with no
    `ssh` binary). A `Native(List(SmartHttp(), SshCommand()))` speaks the wire protocol, version
    2, over both. The command-line backend needs none of this: `git` brings its own transports.

    **Signing** is independent of the backend. A commit or tag is assembled by Soundness and
    stored, rather than made by `git commit`, so the signature never leaves the process: a
    `Git.Signer` makes it, and a `Git.Verifier` checks one. `SshKeygen.Signer(key)` and
    `SshKeygen.Verifier(allowedSigners)` drive `ssh-keygen -Y`, as `gpg.format = ssh` has git
    do; `Gpg.Signer()` and `Gpg.Verifier()` drive `gpg`. A tag signed through either is one
    `git tag -v` accepts.

    The library is split into components, by what each needs from the platform:

    - `octogenarian.core` — the model (`Git` and everything nested in it, `Commit`, `FileDiff`,
      `Hunk`, `Patch`, `Remote`, `ReflogEntry`, `Notes` and `NoteRef`), the `Git.Repo` and
      `Git.Worktree` handles and the `Git.Backend` typeclass they drive. No process is spawned
      here, so a consumer of the native backend never compiles guillotine. JVM and Scala Native;
      on Scala Native, whose javalib has no `java.time`, a new commit is stamped in UTC.
    - `octogenarian.cli` — `Commandline`, the backend which runs `git`, as
      `gitBackends.commandlineGit`; `gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand`; and `Gpg`.
    - `octogenarian.native` — `Native`, the backend which reads and writes the repository's
      files itself, as `gitBackends.soundnessGit`; `Transport` and `Peer`, the wire protocol's
      two ends; `SshAddress`.
    - `octogenarian.http` — `SmartHttp`, remote repositories over smart HTTP(S), as
      `gitTransports.smartHttp`.
    - `octogenarian.ssh` — `SshCommand`, remote repositories over a spawned `ssh`, as
      `gitTransports.sshCommand`; and `SshKeygen`, signing through `ssh-keygen`.
    - `octogenarian.sshNative` — `SshNative`, remote repositories over the native SSH client,
      as `gitTransports.soundnessSsh`.

    None of it compiles for Scala.js, which has no filesystem. Everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella, and the `gitBackends`, `gitCommands` and `gitTransports` choice
    packages merge with the umbrella's own; their givens are imported by name, since
    `import soundness.*` does not import givens. The `NoteRef` extensions `target`,
    `namespace` and `content` are exported too, but `content` is best imported by name
    (`import octogenarian.content`) so that it wins against the generic `read` on paths.

    Whichever backend is chosen, `Exclusive` arbitrates only between handles in this JVM.
    Another process is never waited for, which is why every ref-moving operation is a
    compare-and-swap on the `Stale` reading it was given.

refer octogenarian.Git
refer octogenarian.Git.Repo
refer octogenarian.Git.Worktree
refer octogenarian.Git.Backend
refer octogenarian.gitBackends
refer octogenarian.Commit
refer octogenarian.Native
refer aperture.Stale

entry .Git   the namespace of Git's model and of the repository and worktree handles
  detail
      `Git` holds everything Git itself names: references (`Git.Ref`, `Git.Branch`, `Git.Tag`,
      `Git.Hash`, `Git.Revision`, `Git.Refspec`), objects (`Git.Object` and its kinds), who and
      when (`Git.Identity`, `Git.Timestamp`, `Git.Signature`), the state of HEAD (`Git.Head`),
      the working files (`Git.Status`, `Git.PathStatus`), configuration (`Git.Config`), the
      outcomes of merging and sequencing (`Git.Merge`, `Git.Sequence`, `Git.Operation`), remote
      operations (`Git.Push`, `Git.Pull`, `Git.Activity`), signing (`Git.Signer`,
      `Git.Verifier`, `Git.Verification`), its errors (`Git.Error`, `Git.RefError`), and the two
      forms a path is opened in: `Git.Repo` and `Git.Worktree`, each lending a `Handle` for a
      block. `Git.Backend` is the typeclass a handle drives, implemented by `Commandline` and
      `Native`.

      Three operations create repositories: `.init` makes one, `.clone` copies one from a path
      or a URL, and `.cloneCommit` fetches exactly one commit. Each takes a `Git.Backend^` and a
      `Tactic[Git.Error]`, and the two which reach a remote take an `Internet` as well.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
      Git.init(directory, initialBranch = Git.Branch(t"main"))

      directory.open[Git.Repo](): repo ?=>
        repo.head().value   // Git.Head.Attached(Branch(main), Unset): an unborn branch
      ```
  refer octogenarian.Git.Repo
  refer octogenarian.Git.Worktree
  refer octogenarian.Git.Backend
  refer octogenarian.Git.Error
  refer octogenarian.Commit
  entry .Ref   a fully-qualified reference path, `refs/heads/main`, as a `Path on Git.Refs`
      `Git.Ref` is the alias `Path on Git.Refs`: a reference such as `refs/heads/main`,
      `refs/tags/v1` or `refs/notes/commits`, modelled as a path on the `Git.Refs` plane, so it
      is built with `/` from `Git.Refs`, or with `Git.Refs.heads`, `Git.Refs.tags`,
      `Git.Refs.notes` and `Git.Refs.remotes`, each of which checks its segments as
      `git check-ref-format` would, or parsed from text with `Git.Refs.parse`. A `Git.Branch`
      and a `Git.Tag` each know their own ref (`branch.ref`, `tag.ref`). A `Git.Ref` converts
      to a `Git.Revision` where one is expected, since a validated ref path is already a
      revision.
  entry .Error   raised when a Git operation cannot be completed, with a numbered reason
    detail
        Error 685: _the Git operation could not be completed because `reason`_. The one error
        every repository operation raises, through a `Tactic[Git.Error]` in scope; its
        `#reason` is a `Git.Error.Reason`, numbered so that the full code is `685.<n>`, and its
        `#detail` is what git itself said, when a backend has it: the trimmed standard error of
        the `git` process under `Commandline`, or a filesystem or network failure's own message
        under `Native`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
          val seen = worktree.head()
          worktree.commit(t"First", seen, allowEmpty = true)
          capture[Git.Error](worktree.commit(t"Second", seen, allowEmpty = true)).reason
          // Git.Error.Reason.Moved(t"HEAD", …, …): HEAD moved since `seen` was read
        ```

        The reason which matters most is `Moved`: a write which was conditional on a `Stale`
        reading found the ref elsewhere, and did nothing.
    entry .Reason   why an operation failed: one of thirty-four numbered cases
        A `Clarification` enum, so every case carries its number (`685.<n>`) and renders through
        `.communicable`. The cases, in number order:

        - `CannotExecuteGit` (1): the `git` command could not be executed.
        - `NoRepository(path)` (2): the path is not a Git repository; raised when opening.
        - `NoWorktree` (3): the repository has no working tree, so it cannot be opened as a
          `Git.Worktree`.
        - `Moved(ref, expected, actual)` (4): a compare-and-swap failed — `ref` was `expected`
          when read but is now `actual` (either may be `Unset` for no object). The ref is named
          as the caller used it (`HEAD`), as git does.
        - `NoSuchRef(ref)` (5), `NoSuchObject(hash)` (6), `RefExists(ref)` (7).
        - `Unmerged(branch, tip)` (8): a branch is not fully merged, so `deleteBranch` refused
          without `force`.
        - `Conflict(paths)` (9): there are conflicts in the paths.
        - `NothingToCommit` (10): the tree is unchanged and `allowEmpty` was not given.
        - `Detached` (11): HEAD is detached, where a branch was needed (`pull`).
        - `Dirty(paths)` (12): local changes would be overwritten.
        - `NotFastForward` (13): the update is not a fast-forward.
        - `OutsideWorktree(path)` (14), `Untracked(path)` (15).
        - `NoOperationInProgress` (16), `OperationInProgress(operation)` (17): no merge, rebase,
          cherry-pick or revert is in progress, or one already is.
        - `NoSuchRemote(name)` (18), `RemoteExists(name)` (19), `Unauthorized(remote)` (20),
          `Unreachable(remote)` (21), `RemoteRejected(ref, reason)` (22).
        - `LeaseFailed(ref, expected, actual)` (23): a `Push.Force.Lease` expectation was wrong.
        - `NoteNotFound` (24), `NoteExists(target)` (31).
        - `NoSuchConfig(key)` (25): a configuration key is not set — the committer's
          `user.name` or `user.email`, when a commit is made.
        - `InvalidConfig(key, value)` (26).
        - `BadSignature(detail)` (27), `SigningFailed(detail)` (28).
        - `Unsupported(operation)` (29): the operation is not supported by this backend, or a
          concurrent exclusive open was refused.
        - `Unexpected` (30): git reported a failure the reason table does not know; the message
          is kept as the error's `detail`.
        - `NoSuchWorktree(path)` (32), `NoSuchStash` (33).
        - `Locked(ref)` (34): the lock file for a ref is held by another process.
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   why the operation failed
    entry #detail   what git itself said, if the backend has it: its standard error, trimmed
  entry .RefError   raised when text is not a valid Git reference, hash or refspec
    detail
        Error 976: _`value` is not a valid Git reference because `reason`_. Raised by the
        parsers which check names against git's own rules (`git check-ref-format`):
        `Git.Branch.parse`, `Git.Tag.parse`, `Git.Revision.parse`, `Git.Refs.parse`,
        `Git.Refs.heads` and its siblings, `Git.Hash.apply` and `Git.Refspec.parse`, and the
        `Decodable in Text` givens built from them. `NoteRef#namespace` raises it for a bad
        notes namespace segment.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        safely(Git.Branch.parse(t"feature.lock"))   // Unset: `.lock` is a reserved suffix
        safely(Git.Hash(t"0123456789abcdef"))        // Unset: not forty hexadecimal digits
        ```
    entry .Reason   which rule the text broke
        `LeadingOrTrailingDot` (1): a path segment starts or ends with `.`; `ReservedSuffix`
        (2): a segment ends with `.lock`; `ReservedSequence` (3): the name contains `@{`;
        `DoubleDot` (4): it contains `..`; `EmptySegment` (5): an empty path segment;
        `InvalidCharacter` (6): a character git forbids (`*`, `[`, `\`, space, `^`, `~`, `:`,
        `?`); `BadHash` (7): not a 40-character lowercase hexadecimal hash; `BadRefspec` (8):
        not a valid refspec.
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #value   the text which was rejected
    entry #reason   the rule it broke
  entry .Refs   the plane of fully-qualified references, rooted at `refs/`
    detail
        `Git.Refs` is the root of the plane a `Git.Ref` is a path on: `Git.Refs / "heads" /
        "main"` is `refs/heads/main`. The named constructors check each segment as
        `git check-ref-format` would, raising `Git.RefError`, and `.parse` reads a
        fully-qualified name back from text. The plane's `Filesystem` is `/`-separated, with
        `@` as the self segment and `..` as the parent, neither of which a valid ref contains.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val main: Git.Ref = Git.Refs.heads(t"main")
        val notes: Git.Ref = Git.Refs.notes(t"attestations")
        Git.Refs.parse(t"refs/remotes/origin/main") == Git.Refs.remotes(t"origin", t"main")
        main.encode   // t"refs/heads/main"
        ```

        A `Git.Ref` is accepted wherever a `Git.Revision` is, through `.pathIsRevision`.
    entry .Plane   the plane type of reference paths: `Git.Refs` itself
    entry .notes   `refs/notes/<namespace>`, with the namespace checked
        Raises `Git.RefError` if the namespace breaks a reference rule.
    entry .heads   `refs/heads/<branch>`, with the branch name checked
        Raises `Git.RefError` if the name breaks a reference rule. `Git.Branch#ref` is the same
        path, unchecked.
    entry .tags   `refs/tags/<tag>`, with the tag name checked
    entry .remotes   `refs/remotes/<remote>/<branch>`, with both segments checked
    entry .defaultNotes   `refs/notes/commits`, the namespace git's own `notes` command uses
        The default `ref` of every notes operation on a `Git.Repo.Handle`.
    entry .stash   `refs/stash`, the ref the stash's entries hang from
    entry .parse   reads a fully-qualified name, `refs/heads/main`, as a `Git.Ref`
        The text must begin with `refs/`, and every segment after it must satisfy the
        reference rules; otherwise `Git.RefError` is raised. A name which is not fully
        qualified (`HEAD`, `main`) is rejected.
    entry .validateSegment   checks one segment against git's reference rules, raising `Git.RefError`
        A segment must be non-empty, must not start or end with `.`, must not end with
        `.lock`, must not contain `@{` or `..`, and must contain none of `*`, `[`, `\`, space,
        `^`, `~`, `:`, `?` or `/`.
    entry .admissible   the given which admits any text as a segment of a reference path
        The check is deferred: `/` builds the path, and the named constructors and `.parse`
        validate.
    entry .filesystem   the reference plane as a `Filesystem`: `/`-separated, `@` and `..` for self and parent
    entry .pathIsRevision   a `Git.Ref` as a `Git.Revision`, wherever one is expected
        A `Conversion`, in implicit scope: a validated ref path satisfies every rule git
        enforces, so it is a revision as it stands, and `repo.resolve(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"))`
        needs no wrapping.
  entry .Revision   anything git accepts where a revision is expected: a hash, a branch, a tag or an expression
    detail
        A `Git.Revision` is what `gitrevisions(7)` calls a revision: a `Git.Hash`, a `Git.Branch`
        or a `Git.Tag` (each extends it), a `Git.Ref` (through `Git.Refs.pathIsRevision`), or an
        expression such as `HEAD~2` or `main..feature`. `.head` makes the `HEAD` expressions,
        and `.unsafe` any other, unchecked. Operations which read history or check out take a
        `Git.Revision`: `log`, `resolve`, `checkout`, `merge`, `rebase`, `diffAgainst`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val revisions: List[Git.Revision] =
          List(Git.Revision.head(1), Git.Branch(t"main"), Git.Revision.unsafe(t"main..feature"))

        revisions.map(_.text)   // List(t"HEAD~1", t"main", t"main..feature")
        ```
    entry .head   `HEAD`, or `HEAD~n` for a positive `n`
    entry .unsafe   any text as a revision, unchecked
        For expressions the typed references cannot express, `v1.0^{commit}` or
        `main..feature`. Nothing is validated; an expression git cannot resolve is reported
        when it is used, as `NoSuchRef` or as `Unset` from `resolve`.
    entry .parse   checks a reference *name* against git's rules and returns it
        Validates a name such as `feature/foo` — not an expression — segment by segment, as
        `Git.Refs.validateSegment` does but allowing `/`, and raises `Git.RefError` on the first
        rule broken. `Git.Branch.parse` and `Git.Tag.parse` are built on it.
    entry .encodable   a revision encodes as its text
    entry .showable   a revision shows as its text
    entry .inspectable   a revision inspects as `Revision(HEAD~2)`
        Branches, tags and hashes have their own: `Branch(main)`, `Tag(v1.0)`, `Hash(…)`.
    entry #text   the revision as git takes it
  entry .Refspec   a fetch or push refspec, `+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*`
    detail
        A refspec names a `#source` on one side and a `#destination` on the other, either of
        which may be `Unset`, and whether the update is `#force`d (the leading `+`). A refspec
        with no source and a destination deletes the destination on push. `fetch` and `push`
        take a list of them, and `addRemote` records one as the remote's fetch refspec,
        defaulting to `+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/<name>/*`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Git.Refspec.parse(t"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*")
        // Git.Refspec(t"refs/heads/*", t"refs/remotes/origin/*", true)

        Git.Refspec.parse(t":refs/heads/gone")   // deletes `refs/heads/gone` on push
        Git.Refspec(Git.Refs.heads(t"main")).text   // t"refs/heads/main:refs/heads/main"
        ```
    entry .parse   reads a refspec from text, raising `Git.RefError(BadRefspec)` for one with two colons
    entry .apply   a refspec from one ref (same on both sides) or from a source and a destination ref
        `Git.Refspec(ref)` pushes or fetches `ref` to itself; `Git.Refspec(source, destination)`
        maps one to the other. Neither is forced.
    entry .encodable   a refspec encodes as its text
    entry .showable   a refspec shows as its text
    entry #source   the ref on the local side of a push, or the remote side of a fetch, if any
    entry #destination   the ref on the other side, if any
    entry #force   whether the update may be a non-fast-forward: the leading `+`
    entry #text   the refspec as git takes it: `+source:destination`
  entry .Tag   a tag by its name, distinct in type from a branch or a hash
    detail
        `Git.Tag(t"v1.0")` is a tag name; it is a `Git.Revision`, so it resolves wherever a
        revision is expected, and `#ref` is its full path, `refs/tags/v1.0`. `.parse` checks the
        name against git's rules; the case-class constructor does not, and `.unsafe` says so.
        What a tag points at is a `.Target`, lightweight or annotated, read with
        `Git.Repo.Handle`'s `tag`; an annotated tag's object is an `.Annotation`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
          val hash = worktree.commit(t"Release", worktree.head(), allowEmpty = true)
          worktree.makeTag(Git.Tag(t"v1"), hash)
          worktree.annotatedTag(Git.Tag(t"v1-annotated"), hash, t"Release one")
          worktree.tag(Git.Tag(t"v1")).value   // Git.Tag.Target.Lightweight(hash)
          worktree.tags().value.keys           // Set(Tag(v1), Tag(v1-annotated))
        ```
    entry .unsafe   a tag by its name, unchecked
    entry .parse   a tag by its name, checked against git's reference rules
        Raises `Git.RefError` for a name git would reject.
    entry .decoder   reads a tag from text, through `.parse`
        A `Git.Tag is Decodable in Text`, so `t"v1".as[Git.Tag]` works where a
        `Tactic[Git.RefError]` is in scope.
    entry .showable   a tag shows as its name
    entry .inspectable   a tag inspects as `Tag(v1.0)`
    entry .Target   what a tag name points at: a commit directly, or an annotation which points at one
      detail
          `Lightweight(commit)` is a tag ref which names a commit; `Annotated(annotation,
          commit)` names a tag object, whose own target is `commit`. `#peeled` is the commit
          either way. `Git.Repo.Handle`'s `tag` reads one, and `tags()` lists tags already
          peeled to their commits.
      entry #peeled   the commit the tag ultimately names
    entry .Annotation   the content of an annotated tag object
      detail
          What `annotationAt(hash)` reads back: the object tagged, its kind, the tag's name, who
          tagged it and when (absent in a tag written without a tagger line), the message, and
          the ASCII-armoured signature block if the tag was signed.
      entry #target   the hash of the object tagged
      entry #kind   the kind of the object tagged, usually `Commit`
      entry #name   the tag's name
      entry #tagger   who made the tag and when, if recorded
      entry #message   the tag's message, without git's trailing newline
      entry #signature   the armoured signature block, if the tag is signed
    entry #text   the tag's name
    entry #ref   the tag's full reference, `refs/tags/<name>`
  entry .Branch   a branch by its name, distinct in type from a tag or a hash
    detail
        `Git.Branch(t"main")` is a branch name; it is a `Git.Revision`, so it resolves wherever
        a revision is expected, and `#ref` is its full path, `refs/heads/main`. `.parse` checks
        the name against git's rules; the case-class constructor does not, and `.unsafe` says
        so. Branches are made with `switch(branch, head, create = true)` on a worktree, or
        `makeBranch(branch, at)` on a repository, listed with `branches()`, and deleted or
        renamed against a `Stale` reading of their tip.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val feature: Git.Branch = Git.Branch.parse(t"feature/login")
        feature.ref.encode   // t"refs/heads/feature/login"
        t"main".as[Git.Branch] == Git.Branch(t"main")
        ```
    entry .unsafe   a branch by its name, unchecked
    entry .parse   a branch by its name, checked against git's reference rules
        Raises `Git.RefError` for a name git would reject.
    entry .decoder   reads a branch from text, through `.parse`
        A `Git.Branch is Decodable in Text`, so `t"main".as[Git.Branch]` works where a
        `Tactic[Git.RefError]` is in scope.
    entry .showable   a branch shows as its name
    entry .inspectable   a branch inspects as `Branch(main)`
    entry #text   the branch's name
    entry #ref   the branch's full reference, `refs/heads/<name>`
  entry .Hash   a forty-character object hash, and the root of a notes path
    detail
        A `Git.Hash` is the name of an object: forty lowercase hexadecimal digits, which
        `.apply` checks and `.unsafe` does not. It is a `Git.Revision`, and it is also a
        serpentine `Root`, on the `Notes` plane: `hash / t"attestations"` is a `NoteRef`, the
        note attached to that commit in the `attestations` namespace. Equality is by text, so
        two hashes read from different places compare equal.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val hash: Git.Hash = Git.Hash(t"5c1d2b7e3a4f60918273645566778899aabbccdd")
        hash.text.length          // 40
        hash == Git.Hash.unsafe(t"5c1d2b7e3a4f60918273645566778899aabbccdd")
        (hash / t"attestations").namespace.encode   // t"refs/notes/attestations"
        ```

        Objects are immutable and content-addressed, so a read keyed by a hash (`commitAt`,
        `treeAt`, `blobAt`, `show`) is a plain value, never `Stale`.
    entry .apply   a hash from forty lowercase hexadecimal digits, raising `Git.RefError(BadHash)` otherwise
    entry .unsafe   a hash from text, unchecked
    entry .zero   the all-zero hash, which git uses to mean "no object"
        What `Git.Head#commit.or(Git.Hash.zero)` gives for an unborn branch, and the tip the
        command-line backend reports in `Unmerged(branch, tip)` when git does not say which.
    entry .decoder   reads a hash from text, through `.apply`
        A `Git.Hash is Decodable in Text`, needing a `Tactic[Git.RefError]`.
    entry .showable   a hash shows as its forty digits
    entry .inspectable   a hash inspects as `Hash(5c1d…)`, with all forty digits
    entry #text   the forty hexadecimal digits
    entry #Plane   the plane a hash is the root of: `Notes`
    entry #Limit   the root type of a notes path: `Git.Hash` itself
  entry .Identity   who: a name and an email address
    detail
        `Git.Identity(t"Jon Pretty", t"jon@example.com")` is the `user.name` and `user.email`
        pair a commit's author and committer lines carry. A commit's committer is always read
        from the effective configuration (raising `NoSuchConfig` if either key is unset); its
        author is the same unless `commit(…, author = identity)` says otherwise, and a tag's
        tagger likewise.
    entry #name   the person's name
    entry #email   the person's email address
    entry #text   the identity as git writes it: `name <email>`
  entry .Timestamp   git's own timestamp: seconds since the epoch and a UTC offset in minutes
    detail
        Kept apart from any calendar type so that it round-trips byte-for-byte through the
        objects it is read from and written to: `Git.Timestamp(1700000000, 90).text` is
        `1700000000 +0130`. `.now` reads the wall clock, with the local offset on the JVM and
        `+0000` on Scala Native, whose javalib has no `java.time`.
    entry .now   the current time, with the local UTC offset (always `+0000` on Scala Native)
    entry .parse   a timestamp from its two fields as text, `Unset` if either is malformed
    entry #seconds   seconds since the Unix epoch
    entry #offsetMinutes   the UTC offset in minutes, negative west of Greenwich
    entry #offset   the offset as git writes it: `+0130`, `-0500`
    entry #text   the timestamp as git writes it: `1700000000 +0130`
  entry .Signature   an `author`, `committer` or `tagger` line: who, and when
    detail
        A `Git.Identity` with a `Git.Timestamp`, as `Commit#author`, `Commit#committer` and
        `Git.Tag.Annotation#tagger` carry it. Not to be confused with a cryptographic
        signature, which is a `Git.Signer`'s business.
    entry .parse   a signature from a header line, `Jon <jon@example.com> 1700000000 +0100`, or `Unset`
    entry #identity   who
    entry #timestamp   when
    entry #text   the line as git writes it: `name <email> seconds offset`
  entry .Object   a git object as read from the database: a blob, a tree, a commit or a tag
    detail
        `show(hash)` on a `Git.Repo.Handle` reads any object as a `Git.Object`: `Blob(data)`,
        `Tree(entries)`, `Commit(commit)` or `Tag(annotation)`, with `#kind` naming which. The
        typed readers `blobAt`, `treeAt`, `commitAt` and `annotationAt` read one kind and raise
        `NoSuchObject` for another. The object namespace also holds `.Kind`, the four kinds as
        git names them, and `.Tree`, the modes and entries of a tree.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Repo](): repo ?=>
          val tip = repo.head().value.commit.or(Git.Hash.zero)

          repo.show(tip) match
            case Git.Object.Commit(commit) => commit.message
            case other                     => other.kind.text
        ```
    entry .Kind   the four kinds of object, as git names them: `blob`, `tree`, `commit`, `tag`
      detail
          `Blob`, `Tree`, `Commit` and `Tag`; `.parse` reads one from git's lowercase name and
          `#text` writes it back. `writeObject(kind, data)` takes one to store raw content.
      entry .parse   a kind from `blob`, `tree`, `commit` or `tag`; `Unset` for anything else
      entry #text   the kind as git names it
    entry .Tree   the modes and entries of a tree object
      detail
          A tree is a list of `.Entry`s, each a `.Mode`, a name and the hash of what it names.
          `treeAt(hash)` reads one.
      entry .Mode   the mode of a tree entry: regular, executable, symlink, gitlink or directory
        detail
            `Regular` (`100644`), `Executable` (`100755`), `Symlink` (`120000`), `Gitlink`
            (`160000`, a submodule's commit) and `Directory` (`40000`, a subtree). `.parse` reads
            the octal text git writes, accepting `100664` as regular and `040000` as a
            directory; `#octal` is the canonical spelling.
        entry .parse   a mode from its octal text, `Unset` for one git does not write
        entry #octal   the mode as git writes it in a tree
      entry .Entry   one entry of a tree: its mode, its name and the hash it names
        detail
            `#kind` is what the hash names, by the mode: a `Directory` names a `Tree`, a
            `Gitlink` a `Commit`, and anything else a `Blob`.
        entry #mode   the entry's mode
        entry #name   the entry's name within the tree
        entry #hash   the hash of the object the entry names
        entry #kind   the kind of object the entry names, inferred from its mode
    entry #kind   which kind of object this is
  entry .Head   where HEAD is: attached to a branch, or detached at a commit
    detail
        `Attached(branch, tip)` is HEAD on a branch, whose `tip` is `Unset` on an unborn branch
        (a fresh repository whose first commit has not been made); `Detached(at)` is HEAD at a
        commit. `head()` reads it as a `Stale[Git.Head]`, which is the reading every
        HEAD-moving write (`commit`, `switch`, `checkout`, `reset`, `merge`, `rebase`, …) is
        conditional on.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Repo](): repo ?=>
          repo.head().value match
            case Git.Head.Attached(branch, tip) => t"on $branch"
            case Git.Head.Detached(at)          => t"detached at $at"
        ```
    entry #commit   the commit HEAD is at, `Unset` on an unborn branch
    entry #current   the branch checked out, `Unset` when HEAD is detached
  entry .Status   the state of one path, as a letter of `git status`
    detail
        `Updated` (`M`), `Added` (`A`), `Deleted` (`D`), `Renamed` (`R`), `Copied` (`C`),
        `Unmerged` (`U`), `Untracked` (`?`), `Ignored` (`!`) and `TypeChanged` (`T`). A
        `Git.PathStatus` carries one for the index and one for the working file.
    entry .parse   a status from its `git status --porcelain` letter, `Unset` for a space or anything else
  entry .PathStatus   one line of `status`: the index's and the working file's state, and the path
    detail
        What `status()` on a `Git.Worktree.Handle` lists, one per path: the state of the index
        relative to HEAD, the state of the working file relative to the index (each `Unset`
        where unchanged), the path, and, for a rename or copy, where it came from. The paths
        are on the worktree handle's plane, so they cannot outlive the handle and can be read
        and written through it.
    entry #index   the index's state relative to HEAD, if changed
    entry #worktree   the working file's state relative to the index, if changed
    entry #path   the path, on the worktree handle's plane
    entry #origin   where a renamed or copied path came from
  entry .Activity   the progress of a clone, fetch, push or pull, shared between the worker and a watcher
    detail
        A long-running remote operation blocks until it is done and reports its progress
        through an `Activity`: a handful of atomic cells — the phase, whether the remote is
        reporting it, how much is done of how much, and the bytes transferred — each updated
        indivisibly, so no other thread sees a half-done state. Updating is allocation-free: a
        backend bumps the counters as a sideband line arrives or an object is processed. A user
        interface polls `#report` at its own cadence, or, for a reactive one, drains `#updates`,
        a `Relay` which exists only once asked for, so a poll-only caller pays nothing for it.

        The operations take `activity: Optional[Activity^] = Unset` and make a private one when
        none is given. A caller who wants to watch makes one and passes it:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.cancelProbate

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
        val activity: Git.Activity^ = Git.Activity()

        supervise:
          val watcher = async:
            activity.updates.chain.each: report =>
              Out.println(t"${report.phase}: ${report.complete.or(0.0)*100}%")

          Git.clone(t"https://github.com/propensive/soundness", directory, activity = activity)
          watcher.await()

        activity.complete   // true
        ```

        `Activity` is `Durable`: it may outlive the scope which made it, since the watching
        thread holds it after the operation returns.
    entry .Phase   which stage of a transfer is under way
      detail
          `Idle`, then git's own stages — `Enumerating`, `Counting`, `Compressing`, `Writing`,
          `Receiving`, `Resolving`, `Unpacking` — then `Checkout` and `Done`. `.parse` reads
          one from the heading of a git progress line (`Receiving objects`).
      entry .parse   a phase from git's progress heading, `Receiving objects`; `Unset` for an unknown one
    entry .Report   one observation of an activity: pure data, stale as soon as it is read
      detail
          The phase, whether the remote end is reporting it (`remote:` lines), how many items
          are done, how many there are in all if known, and the bytes transferred so far.
          `#complete` is the fraction done, or `Unset` while the total is unknown.
      entry #phase   the stage under way
      entry #remote   whether the other end of the transfer is the one reporting
      entry #done   how many objects or files are done in this phase
      entry #total   how many there are in all, once known
      entry #bytes   the bytes transferred so far
      entry #complete   `done/total` as a fraction, `1.0` for an empty phase, `Unset` while the total is unknown
    entry #report   the current state as a `Stale[Git.Activity.Report]`
        Reads every cell once; the result is a consistent snapshot of a moment, already out of
        date when it returns.
    entry #complete   whether the operation has finished
    entry #updates   a `Relay` of reports, one per change, ending when the activity finishes
        Created on the first request and fed from then on; a caller who never asks pays
        nothing. Drain its stream from another thread.
    entry #begin   starts a phase, resetting the counts; a backend calls it
    entry #measure   sets the phase's total; a backend calls it
    entry #advance   adds to the phase's done count; a backend calls it
    entry #progress   sets the done count and the total together; a backend calls it
    entry #transferred   adds to the bytes transferred; a backend calls it
    entry #finish   marks the activity done and stops the `#updates` relay; a backend calls it
  entry .Config   git configuration: keys, scopes, sources, and the handle which reads and writes them
    detail
        Configuration is read and written through a lent `Git.Config.Handle`, typed by the
        value's codec: `repo.config { config ?=> config.get[Text](key) }` reads the repository's
        own file, `repo.configure { … }` writes it, `repo.effectiveConfig { … }` reads through
        every scope git would consult, and any file opens on its path as
        `path.open[Git.Config](Read & Write) { config ?=> … }` — the global `~/.gitconfig`,
        say. A key is a `.Key`, parsed from `section.name` or `section.subsection.name`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
        val key = Git.Config.Key.parse(t"octogenarian.answer")

        directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
          worktree.configure { config ?=> config.set(key, 42) }
          worktree.config { config ?=> config.get[Int](key).value }   // 42
        ```

        A read is `Stale`, since the file may be rewritten by another process; `get` of an
        unset key is `Unset`, not an error.
    entry .Scope   where a setting lives: `Local`, `Worktree`, `Global` or `System`
    entry .Key   a configuration key: its section, optional subsection and name
      detail
          `Git.Config.Key(t"remote", t"origin", t"url")` is `remote.origin.url`; `.parse` reads
          the dotted form, lowercasing the section and the name (the subsection is
          case-sensitive, as git has it) and raising `Git.Error(InvalidConfig)` for text with no
          section or no name.
      entry .parse   a key from `section.name` or `section.subsection.name`, raising `Git.Error(InvalidConfig)` otherwise
      entry .showable   a key shows as its dotted text
      entry #section   the section, lowercase
      entry #subsection   the subsection, if there is one
      entry #name   the name, lowercase
      entry #text   the key in dotted form
    entry .Source   the configuration file a handle reads and writes
      detail
          `Repository(gitDir, scope)` is a repository's own file in a scope; `File(path)` is a
          named file, such as `~/.gitconfig`; `Effective(gitDir)` reads through every scope git
          would consult for the repository, and cannot be written. The handle's `#source` is
          one of these; a `Git.Backend`'s `configRead`, `configWrite` and `configUnset` take one.
    entry .Handle   a lent configuration handle: `get`, `all` and `list` to read, `set`, `add` and `unset` to write
      detail
          Lent for a block by `config`, `configure` or `effectiveConfig` on a repository handle,
          or by opening a file as `Git.Config`. It is an exclusive capability, so it cannot be
          kept beyond the block. The reads need `Grant.Read` and the writes `Grant.Write`, as
          the mode the handle was opened with grants them; a write through a read-only handle
          does not compile.
      entry .get   the value in force for a key, decoded, as `Stale[Optional[value]]`
          Needs a `value is Decodable in Text`. Git lists scopes from system to local and the
          last setting wins, which is what this returns; an unset key is `Unset`.
      entry .all   every value set for a multi-valued key, decoded, as `Stale[List[value]]`
      entry .list   every key and its raw value, as `Stale[List[(Git.Config.Key, Text)]]`
      entry .set   sets a key to a value, encoded, replacing any value it had
          Needs a `value is Encodable in Text` and `Grant.Write`.
      entry .add   adds a value to a key, keeping the values it had
      entry .unset   removes a key's value, or with `all = true` every value of a multi-valued key
      entry #source   the file or scope this handle reads and writes
    entry .Openable   how a path opens as a `Git.Config`: a lent handle on that file
      detail
          The `aperture.Openable` behind `path.open[Git.Config](mode) { config ?=> … }`: the
          handle's source is `Git.Config.Source.File(path)`. It needs a `Git.Backend^` and a
          `Tactic[Git.Error]` when built, which `.openable` supplies from scope.
      entry #open   opens the path, lending a handle with the mode's grants to the block
      entry #Self   the path type opened
      entry #Form   the form opened in: `Git.Config`
      entry #Operand   the flag type: none
      entry #Result   what is lent: a `Git.Config.Handle`
    entry .openable   the given which lets any `Path on Linux` open as `Git.Config`
        In implicit scope; it needs a `Git.Backend^` (from `gitBackends`) and a
        `Tactic[Git.Error]`.
  entry .Operation   an operation stopped part-way, waiting for conflicts to be resolved
    detail
        What `.git` records about a merge, rebase, cherry-pick or revert which stopped, or a
        bisection in progress: `Merging(merging, conflicting)`, `Rebasing(onto, originalHead,
        done, remaining, conflicting)`, `CherryPicking(commit, conflicting)`,
        `Reverting(commit, conflicting)` and `Bisecting`. A `Stopped` outcome carries one, and
        `operation()` on a repository handle reads whichever is in progress. `#conflicts` names
        the paths which must be resolved and added before `continue()`.
    entry #conflicts   the paths left in conflict, which must be resolved before the operation continues
  entry .Sequence   the outcome of a cherry-pick, revert, rebase or stash application, and the verbs which drive one
    detail
        `.Outcome` is what each of those returns: `Completed(head)` with the new HEAD, or
        `Stopped(operation)` on a conflict, which is not an error. Once the conflicts are
        resolved and added, `continue()` finishes the operation, `skip()` passes over the
        current commit, `abandon()` restores the state it began from, and `quit()` leaves the
        tree as it is; these are the `.Verb`s a backend's `sequence` takes.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
          worktree.rebase(Git.Branch(t"main"), worktree.head()) match
            case Git.Sequence.Outcome.Completed(head)    => t"rebased onto $head"
            case Git.Sequence.Outcome.Stopped(operation) =>
              t"conflicts in ${operation.conflicts.join(t", ")}"
        ```
    entry .Outcome   `Completed(head)`, or `Stopped(operation)` on a conflict
    entry .Verb   `Continue`, `Skip`, `Abort` or `Quit`: how an operation in progress is driven
  entry .Merge   the outcome of a merge or a pull
    detail
        `.Outcome` is `FastForwarded(head)` when the branch simply advanced, `Merged(head)`
        when a merge commit was made, `UpToDate` when there was nothing to do, or
        `Stopped(operation)` on a conflict, which is not an error: resolve the paths, add them,
        and `continue()`.
    entry .Outcome   `FastForwarded(head)`, `Merged(head)`, `UpToDate` or `Stopped(operation)`
  entry .Rebase   the options a rebase takes
    entry .Option   `Autosquash`, `KeepEmpty`, `NoRenames`, `Signoff` or `Autostash`
  entry .Pull   how a pull integrates what it fetched
    entry .Mode   `Merge`, `Rebase` or `FastForwardOnly`
        `Merge` merges `FETCH_HEAD` as `FastForward.Auto` would; `FastForwardOnly` refuses
        anything but a fast-forward; `Rebase` rebases the branch onto it, reporting a
        completed rebase as `Merged`.
  entry .Push   the force policy of a push, and what happened to each ref
    detail
        `.Force` says whether a non-fast-forward update is allowed: `Never`, `Always`, or
        `Lease(expectations)`, git's `--force-with-lease` — the remote-side compare-and-swap,
        carrying the `Stale` readings of the remote's refs the push is conditional on, as
        `remoteRefs` gave them. `.Outcome` is what happened to each ref pushed.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
        val main = Git.Refs.heads(t"main")

        directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
          val seen = worktree.remoteRefs(t"origin").let(_.at(main))
          val lease = Git.Push.Force.Lease(Map(main -> seen))
          worktree.push(t"origin", List(Git.Refspec(main)), force = lease).value.at(main)
        ```
    entry .Force   `Never`, `Always`, or `Lease(expectations)` for `--force-with-lease`
    entry .Outcome   `Updated(from, to, forced)`, `Created(to)`, `Deleted(from)`, `UpToDate` or `Rejected(reason)`
        A failed lease is reported as `Rejected`, per ref, rather than raised.
  entry .Stash   the entries of the stash
    detail
        `stashes()` on a worktree handle lists them as `Stale[List[Git.Stash.Entry]]`, newest
        first; `popStash`, `applyStash` and `dropStash` take a `Stale[Git.Stash.Entry]` and
        raise `NoSuchStash` if the entry has gone or changed since it was listed.
    entry .Entry   one stash entry: its index, its commit and its message
      entry #index   the entry's position, `0` for the newest
      entry #commit   the commit which holds the stashed state
      entry #message   the entry's message
  entry .Signer   makes the signature a commit or a tag carries
    detail
        A `Git.Signer` produces an ASCII-armoured signature over a commit's or tag's payload,
        which the handle inserts as the `gpgsig` header of a commit or the trailing block of a
        tag. Pass one as `commit(…, signer = …)`, `amend(…, signer = …)` or `annotatedTag(…,
        signer = …)`. `SshKeygen.Signer(key)` signs through `ssh-keygen -Y sign`, and
        `Gpg.Signer()` through `gpg`; signing never leaves the process, since the object is
        assembled here rather than by `git commit`.
    entry #sign   the armoured signature over a payload, raising `Git.Error(SigningFailed)` if it cannot be made
  entry .Verifier   checks the signature a commit or a tag carries
    detail
        A `Git.Verifier` in scope is what `verifyCommit` and `verifyTag` use.
        `SshKeygen.Verifier(allowedSigners)` checks against an allowed-signers file, and
        `Gpg.Verifier()` against the keyring.
    entry #verify   the `Git.Verification` of a signature over a payload
  entry .Verification   `Good(signer)`, `Bad(detail)` or `Unknown(detail)`
      The result of verifying a commit or tag: `Good` names the principal or user id the
      signature was made by; `Bad` means the signature does not match; `Unknown` means it could
      not be checked — the object is unsigned, the tag is not annotated, or the signer is not
      among the allowed ones.
  entry .Backend   the implementation a repository handle drives: `Commandline` or `Native`
    detail
        Every operation on a `Git.Repo.Handle` or a `Git.Worktree.Handle` is written once, over
        the primitives this trait declares; the two backends implement them differently. The
        plumbing — refs, objects, the index, configuration, notes — is simple enough that
        compare-and-swap, object assembly and signing live in the handle; the algorithmic
        verbs (`merge`, `rebase`, `cherryPick`, `stash`, the transports) are each backend's
        own. A `Git.Backend^` is a capability: it is chosen by importing one of the
        `gitBackends` givens by name, and every handle keeps the one it was opened with.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging

        val backend: Git.Backend^ = commandlineGit
        ```

        The members are the plumbing a handle calls; a program drives a repository through the
        handle's own operations rather than these. `Unsupported(operation)` is what a backend
        raises for a primitive it does not implement.
    refer octogenarian.gitBackends
    refer octogenarian.Commandline
    refer octogenarian.Native
    entry .Location   where a repository was found: its git directory, the shared one, and its working tree
      detail
          What `#locate` returns. `#commonDir` is the same as `#gitDir` unless the path is a
          linked worktree, whose own git directory names the shared one; `#worktree` is `Unset`
          for a bare repository.
      entry #gitDir   the repository's own git directory
      entry #commonDir   the git directory shared by every worktree, where the objects and refs are
      entry #worktree   the root of the working tree, if the repository has one
    entry .Expectation   what a ref must hold for an update to proceed: anything, nothing, or one hash
      detail
          `Unconditional` overwrites whatever the ref holds; `Absent` requires the ref not to
          exist (how a branch or tag is created); `Exactly(hash)` requires it to hold that
          hash. `.apply` lifts an `Optional[Git.Hash]` — a `Stale` reading's value — to `Absent`
          or `Exactly`, which is how every compare-and-swap is spelled. It is an enum rather
          than an `Optional[Optional[Git.Hash]]`, which would collapse.
      entry .apply   `Absent` for `Unset`, otherwise `Exactly(hash)`
      entry #expected   the hash required, or `Unset`
    entry .Target   a ref's content: `Direct(hash)` or `Symbolic(ref)`
    entry .StatusLine   one line of status with its paths as text, before they are typed to a handle's plane
      entry #index   the index's state relative to HEAD, if changed
      entry #worktree   the working file's state relative to the index, if changed
      entry #path   the path, relative to the working tree
      entry #origin   where a renamed or copied path came from
    entry .NoteEdit   one edit to a notes tree: `Add`, `Append`, `Remove` or `Copy`
        What `#editNotes` applies: `Add(target, body, force)`, `Append(target, body)`,
        `Remove(target, ignoreMissing)` or `Copy(source, target, force)`.
    entry .WorktreeEntry   one linked worktree as a backend lists it; `Git.Worktree.Entry` is the handle's form
      entry #path   the worktree's root
      entry #head   the commit checked out, if any
      entry #branch   the branch checked out, if attached
      entry #bare   whether the entry is the bare repository itself
      entry #locked   the lock's reason, if the worktree is locked
      entry #prunable   why the worktree could be pruned, if it could
    entry #locate   where the repository containing a path is, or `Unset` if there is none
    entry #init   creates a repository at a path, with an initial branch, bare or not
    entry #clone   clones a source (a path or a URL) to a path, reporting through an activity
    entry #head   where HEAD is in a git directory
    entry #readRef   a ref's content, direct or symbolic, or `Unset` if it does not exist
    entry #listRefs   every ref under a prefix with its hash
    entry #peel   the commit a tag object ultimately names
    entry #updateRef   moves or deletes a ref, conditional on an `Expectation`, with a reflog message
        The compare-and-swap every ref-moving operation rests on: raises `Moved` when the ref
        does not hold what was expected, and `Locked` when another process holds its lock.
    entry #updateSymbolicRef   points a symbolic ref such as `HEAD` at another ref
    entry #resolve   the hash a revision expression names, or `Unset`
    entry #revList   the commits reachable from a revision, newest first, optionally bounded, limited or filtered by path
    entry #reflog   the reflog of a ref, or of `HEAD`
    entry #mergeBase   the best common ancestor of two commits, if any
    entry #isAncestor   whether one commit is reachable from another
    entry #readObject   an object's kind and raw content, or `Unset`
    entry #readObjects   several objects' hashes, kinds and content at once
    entry #writeObject   stores raw content as an object of a kind, returning its hash
    entry #diffTree   the file differences between two trees
    entry #status   the state of the index and working files, as status lines
    entry #add   stages paths, or everything with `all`
    entry #remove   removes paths from the index, and from the working tree unless `cached`
    entry #move   renames a path in the index and the working tree
    entry #restore   restores paths in the index or the working tree from a source
    entry #clean   deletes untracked files, returning the paths removed
    entry #writeTree   writes the index as a tree object, returning its hash
    entry #readTree   brings the index (and, for `Hard`, the working files) into line with HEAD, as a `ResetMode` says
    entry #diffWorktree   the working files or the index against HEAD or a revision
    entry #tracked   every path the index tracks
    entry #conflicts   every path in conflict
    entry #checkout   checks a revision out, detaching HEAD
    entry #switch   switches to a branch, creating it first if asked
    entry #merge   merges a revision into HEAD under a fast-forward policy
    entry #cherryPick   applies commits onto HEAD
    entry #revert   applies the inverse of commits onto HEAD
    entry #rebase   rebases HEAD onto a revision, with options
    entry #sequence   continues, skips, aborts or quits the operation in progress
    entry #operation   the operation in progress, if any
    entry #stash   saves the uncommitted state as a stash entry, returning its commit, or `Unset` if there was nothing
    entry #stashes   the stash's entries, newest first
    entry #applyStash   applies a stash entry, restoring the index too if asked, and drops it if asked
    entry #dropStash   deletes a stash entry
    entry #readNote   the note on a target in a notes ref, if any
    entry #listNotes   every note in a notes ref, as pairs of note blob and target
    entry #editNotes   makes a new notes commit from a base commit and an edit, on no ref
        The handle then moves the notes ref to it by compare-and-swap, which is what keeps two
        writers from clobbering each other's notes.
    entry #configRead   every key and value of a configuration source
    entry #configWrite   sets a key, or adds a value to it
    entry #configUnset   removes a key's value, or all of them
    entry #remoteRefs   every ref a remote advertises, with its hash
    entry #fetch   fetches refspecs from a remote, returning the refs updated
    entry #push   pushes refspecs to a remote under a force policy, returning each ref's outcome
    entry #worktrees   every linked worktree
    entry #addWorktree   adds a linked worktree at a path, checked out at a revision
    entry #removeWorktree   removes a linked worktree
    entry #lockWorktree   locks a linked worktree, with a reason
    entry #unlockWorktree   unlocks a linked worktree
    entry #moveWorktree   moves a linked worktree to another path
    entry #pruneWorktrees   removes the records of worktrees whose directories have gone, returning their paths
  entry .OpenFlag   flags an open may take: `Await`
      `Await` waits for a conflicting exclusive handle in this JVM to close, rather than
      failing at once. Passed as `path.open[Git.Repo](Exclusive & Write, Git.OpenFlag.Await)`.
      Exclusivity is arbitrated only among handles in this JVM, through the same register as
      galilei's directory handles; another process is never waited for.
  entry .Repo   the form a path opens in as a repository: its object database and refs, lending a `Handle`
    detail
        `path.open[Git.Repo](mode) { repo ?=> … }` opens a bare repository, a `.git` directory,
        a working tree containing one, or a linked worktree's `.git` file, and lends a
        `Git.Repo.Handle` to the block. The mode names the grants: `Read` (the default, with
        `open[Git.Repo]()`), `Read & Write`, or `Exclusive & Write` to refuse other exclusive
        handles in this JVM (with `Git.OpenFlag.Await` to wait for them instead). Opening
        verifies that a repository is there — raising `Git.Error(NoRepository(path))` otherwise
        — so a handle always denoted one when it was granted; what happens afterwards is
        another process's business, which is why every read which resolves a ref is `Stale`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Repo](Read & Write): repo ?=>
          val tip = repo.head().value.commit.or(Git.Hash.zero)
          repo.makeBranch(Git.Branch(t"release"), tip)
          repo.branches().value.keys
        ```

        The handle is an exclusive capability: it cannot be kept beyond the block, nor can
        anything derived from it. Every operation is an extension on the handle gated by a
        grant — the reads on `Git.Repo.Handle & Granting[Grant.Read]`, the writes on `…
        & Granting[Grant.Write]` — so a write on a handle opened without `Write` is a
        compile-time error. A `Git.Worktree.Handle` is a `Git.Repo.Handle`, so all of this is
        available on a worktree too.
    refer octogenarian.Git.Worktree
    refer octogenarian.Git.Backend
    refer aperture.Stale
    entry .Handle   a lent repository handle: every repository operation is an extension on it, gated by grant
      detail
          The reads which resolve a ref, HEAD, a revision expression, the reflog, the stash,
          configuration, remotes or linked worktrees return a `Stale` value: true at the moment
          of the read, and perhaps not since. A read keyed by a hash (`.show`, `.commitAt`,
          `.treeAt`, `.blobAt`, `.annotationAt`, `.mergeBase`, `.isAncestor`, `.exists`) is a
          plain value, since objects are immutable and content-addressed.

          Every write which moves a ref takes the `Stale` reading it is conditional on and
          raises `Git.Error(Moved(ref, expected, actual))` if the ref no longer holds it: a
          compare-and-swap, the only defence against another process writing the same
          repository. Creating a branch or a tag is conditional on its absence instead, so
          `.makeBranch` and `.makeTag` take no reading and raise `Moved(ref, Unset, actual)` if
          the name exists.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*
          import gitBackends.commandlineGit
          import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
          import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
          import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
          import logging.silentLogging
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
          ```

          ```scala fuel
          frame
            label A reading of a ref is proof of what was seen
            code
                directory.open[Git.Repo](Read & Write): repo ?=>
                  val seen: Stale[Optional[Git.Hash]] = repo.ref(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"))
          frame
            label A write is conditional on it
            code
                directory.open[Git.Repo](Read & Write): repo ?=>
                  val seen: Stale[Optional[Git.Hash]] = repo.ref(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"))
                  val tip = repo.resolve(Git.Branch(t"feature")).value
                  repo.updateRef(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"), tip, seen, t"advance to feature")
          frame
            label If the ref moved in between, nothing is overwritten
            code
                directory.open[Git.Repo](Read & Write): repo ?=>
                  val seen: Stale[Optional[Git.Hash]] = repo.ref(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"))
                  val tip = repo.resolve(Git.Branch(t"feature")).value
                  repo.updateRef(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"), tip, seen, t"advance to feature")

                  capture[Git.Error](repo.updateRef(Git.Refs.heads(t"main"), tip, seen)).reason
                  // Moved(t"refs/heads/main", …, …): `seen` is no longer what the ref holds
          ##
          ```

          The handle's own fields are where the repository is: `#gitDir`, `#commonDir`,
          `#worktree` and the `#atoms` of the mode it was opened with.
      entry .head   where HEAD is, as a `Stale[Git.Head]`
          The reading every HEAD-moving write on a worktree is conditional on.
      entry .ref   the hash a ref holds, following symbolic refs, as `Stale[Optional[Git.Hash]]`
          `Unset` for a ref which does not exist. A reading of `Git.Refs.defaultNotes` is what
          the notes writes are conditional on.
      entry .refs   every ref under a prefix (or all of them) with its hash, as a `Stale` map
          Names which are not fully-qualified refs (`HEAD`) are dropped.
      entry .symbolicRef   what a symbolic ref such as `HEAD` points to, as text, or `Unset` if it is not symbolic
      entry .resolve   the hash a revision names, or `Unset` if git cannot resolve it
          Takes any `Git.Revision`: a branch, a tag, a hash, a `Git.Ref`, or an expression such
          as `Git.Revision.head(2)`. An unknown revision is `Unset`, not an error.
      entry .branches   every branch with its tip, as a `Stale` map
      entry .tags   every tag with the commit it names, peeled, as a `Stale` map
          An annotated tag is peeled to the commit its tag object points at; `.tag` reads the
          unpeeled `Git.Tag.Target`.
      entry .tag   what a tag points at, lightweight or annotated, or `Unset` if there is no such tag
      entry .log   the commits reachable from a revision, newest first, as a `Stale[List[Commit]]`
          From `HEAD` unless `from` is given; at most `limit` commits; only those touching
          `paths` if any are named. An unborn branch has no history and gives `Nil`; any other
          unresolvable start raises `NoSuchRef`.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*
          import gitBackends.commandlineGit
          import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
          import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
          import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
          import logging.silentLogging
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

          directory.open[Git.Repo](): repo ?=>
            repo.log(limit = 10).value.map(_.message)
          ```
      entry .revList   the hashes reachable from a revision, newest first, optionally stopping at `until`, as a `Stale` list
      entry .reflog   the local history of where a ref has pointed, newest first, as a `Stale[List[ReflogEntry]]`
          Of `HEAD` unless `ref` is given: the record which makes a mistaken reset recoverable.
      entry .mergeBase   the best common ancestor of two commits, or `Unset` if they share none
      entry .isAncestor   whether one commit is reachable from another
      entry .exists   whether an object with a hash is in the database
      entry .show   any object by its hash, as a `Git.Object`; raises `NoSuchObject` if absent
      entry .commitAt   the commit with a hash, as a `Commit`; raises `NoSuchObject` for any other object
          Named `commitAt`, not `commit`, since `commit` is the worktree's write verb and a
          grant-gated handle cannot overload.
      entry .treeAt   the entries of the tree with a hash; raises `NoSuchObject` for any other object
      entry .blobAt   the content of the blob with a hash, as `Data`; raises `NoSuchObject` for any other object
      entry .annotationAt   the annotated tag object with a hash; raises `NoSuchObject` for any other object
      entry .verifyCommit   checks a commit's signature with the `Git.Verifier` in scope
          `Unknown("the commit is not signed")` for an unsigned commit; `Good(signer)` or
          `Bad(detail)` otherwise. Needs a `Git.Verifier` given, such as
          `SshKeygen.Verifier(allowedSigners)` or `Gpg.Verifier()`.
      entry .verifyTag   checks a tag's signature with the `Git.Verifier` in scope, as a `Stale[Git.Verification]`
          `Stale`, since a tag name is a ref. `Unknown` for a lightweight tag or an unsigned
          annotation.
      entry .config   lends the repository's own configuration for a block, read-only
          `repo.config { config ?=> config.get[Text](key).value }`. Global and system
          configuration are opened on their files instead: with `gitconfig: Path on Linux`
          the user's own file, `gitconfig.open[Git.Config](Read) { config ?=> … }`.
      entry .effectiveConfig   lends every scope git would consult for this repository, read-only
          System, global, local and worktree configuration together, the last setting winning,
          as git reads them. Cannot be written.
      entry .note   the note attached to a commit in a notes ref, as `Stale[Optional[Text]]`
          In `Git.Refs.defaultNotes` (`refs/notes/commits`) unless `ref` is given; `Unset`
          where there is no note.
      entry .notes   every note in a notes ref, as a `Stale` map from annotated commit to note blob
      entry .remotes   every remote, read from the repository's configuration, as a `Stale[List[Remote]]`
      entry .remote   one remote by its name, as `Stale[Optional[Remote]]`
      entry .remoteRefs   every ref a remote advertises, with its hash, as a `Stale` map
          Needs an `Internet`. The readings are what a `Git.Push.Force.Lease` carries.
      entry .upstream   the remote and branch a branch tracks, if configured, as a `Stale` pair
      entry .worktrees   every linked worktree, as a `Stale[List[Git.Worktree.Entry]]`
          Each entry is the reading the worktree writes (`.removeWorktree`, `.lockWorktree`,
          `.unlockWorktree`, `.moveWorktree`) take.
      entry .operation   the merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert or bisection in progress, if any, as a `Stale`
      entry .updateRef   moves or deletes a ref, conditional on a `Stale` reading of it
          `updateRef(ref, to, from, message)`: sets `ref` to `to` (or deletes it, for `Unset`)
          if it still holds `from.value`, raising `Moved` otherwise; `message` is the reflog
          line. Needs `Grant.Write`.
      entry .forceRef   moves or deletes a ref unconditionally, overwriting whatever it holds
      entry .updateSymbolicRef   points a symbolic ref such as `HEAD` at a `Git.Ref`
      entry .writeObject   stores raw content as an object of a kind, returning its hash
      entry .makeBranch   creates a branch at a commit, without checking it out; raises `Moved` if it exists
          Creation is conditional on the branch's absence, so no reading is taken. To create
          and check out in one step, `switch(branch, head, create = true)` on a worktree.
      entry .deleteBranch   deletes a branch, conditional on a reading of its tip; refuses an unmerged one unless forced
          Raises `Unmerged(branch, tip)` if the tip is not an ancestor of HEAD and `force` is
          false, and `Moved` if the branch no longer holds `at.value`.
      entry .renameBranch   renames a branch, conditional on a reading of its tip; HEAD follows if it was attached
      entry .makeTag   creates a lightweight tag at a commit; raises `Moved` if it exists
      entry .annotatedTag   writes an annotated tag object with a message, optionally signed, and returns its hash
          `annotatedTag(tag, at, message, tagger, signer)`: the tagger is the configured
          identity unless given, stamped now; a `Git.Signer` appends an armoured signature,
          which `git tag -v` accepts. The tag ref is created conditional on its absence.
      entry .deleteTag   deletes a tag, conditional on a reading of its object (annotated or not, as `tags()` reports it)
      entry .configure   lends the repository's own configuration for a block, for reading and writing
          `repo.configure { config ?=> config.set(key, value) }`. Needs `Grant.Write`.
      entry .addNote   attaches a note to a commit, conditional on a reading of the notes ref; returns the notes commit
          `addNote(target, body, from, ref, force)`: `from` is `repo.ref(ref)`, the reading of
          the notes ref (`Git.Refs.defaultNotes` unless `ref` is given). Raises `NoteExists`
          if a note is already attached and `force` is false, and `Moved` if the notes ref moved
          since `from` was read — which is how build attestations and other out-of-band
          metadata travel with a repository without one writer clobbering another.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*
          import gitBackends.commandlineGit
          import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
          import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
          import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
          import logging.silentLogging
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

          directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
            val hash = worktree.head().value.commit.or(Git.Hash.zero)
            val notes = worktree.ref(Git.Refs.defaultNotes)
            worktree.addNote(hash, t"a note body", notes)
            worktree.note(hash).value   // t"a note body"
          ```
      entry .appendNote   appends to the note on a commit, conditional on a reading of the notes ref
      entry .removeNote   removes the note on a commit, conditional on a reading of the notes ref
          Raises `NoteNotFound` if there is none, unless `ignoreMissing` is true.
      entry .copyNote   copies the note on one commit to another, conditional on a reading of the notes ref
      entry .addRemote   adds a remote by name and URL, with a fetch refspec; raises `RemoteExists`
          The refspec defaults to `+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/<name>/*`. The URL may be a
          local path, which both backends reach without a transport.
      entry .removeRemote   removes a remote's configuration and its `refs/remotes/<name>/` refs; raises `NoSuchRemote`
      entry .renameRemote   renames a remote, rewriting its refspec, its tracking refs and the branches which track it
      entry .setRemoteUrl   sets a remote's fetch URL, or with `push = true` its push URL
      entry .fetch   fetches from a remote, returning the refs updated as a `Stale` map
          `fetch(remote, refspecs, depth, prune, tags, activity)`: the remote's configured
          refspec unless `refspecs` are given; shallow to `depth` commits if given; `prune`
          deletes tracking refs the remote no longer has; `tags` fetches every tag. Needs an
          `Internet`; progress goes to the `activity` if one is passed.
      entry .push   pushes to a remote under a force policy, returning each ref's outcome as a `Stale` map
          `push(remote, refspecs, force, tags, setUpstream, activity)`: `force` is
          `Git.Push.Force.Never` unless given, and `Lease(expectations)` makes the push
          conditional on readings of the remote's refs. A rejected ref is a `Rejected` outcome,
          not an error. Needs an `Internet`.
      entry .addWorktree   adds a linked worktree at a path, checked out at a revision, returning the path
          `addWorktree(target, revision, detach, newBranch)`: open the returned path as a
          `Git.Worktree` afterwards. The target may be any path type.
      entry .removeWorktree   removes a linked worktree, conditional on a reading of its entry
      entry .lockWorktree   locks a linked worktree against pruning, with a reason
      entry .unlockWorktree   unlocks a linked worktree
      entry .moveWorktree   moves a linked worktree to another path, returning the new path
      entry .pruneWorktrees   removes the records of worktrees whose directories have gone, returning their paths
      entry #gitDir   the repository's own git directory
      entry #commonDir   the git directory shared by every worktree, where the objects and refs are
          The same as `#gitDir` unless the handle was opened on a linked worktree.
      entry #worktree   the root of the working tree, if the repository has one
      entry #atoms   the atoms of the mode the handle was opened with
    entry .Openable   how a path opens as a `Git.Repo`: locates the repository and lends a `Handle`
      detail
          The `aperture.Openable` behind `path.open[Git.Repo](mode, flags) { repo ?=> … }`. It
          asks the backend to locate the repository (raising `NoRepository`), registers the
          open when the mode is `Exclusive`, and lends a `Git.Repo.Handle` with the mode's
          grants. `.openable` builds one from the `Git.Backend^` and `Tactic[Git.Error]` in
          scope.
      entry #open   opens the path, lending a handle with the mode's grants to the block
      entry #Self   the path type opened
      entry #Form   the form opened in: `Git.Repo`
      entry #Operand   the flag type: `Git.OpenFlag`
      entry #Result   what is lent: a `Git.Repo.Handle`
    entry .openable   the given which lets any `Path on Linux` open as `Git.Repo`
        In implicit scope; it needs a `Git.Backend^` (import one from `gitBackends`) and a
        `Tactic[Git.Error]`. Since every `Path on Linux` has it, and `Git.Worktree.openable`
        too, the form is always named: `open[Git.Repo]` or `open[Git.Worktree]`.
  entry .Worktree   the form a path opens in as a checked-out tree: the index and working files over a repository
    detail
        `path.open[Git.Worktree](mode) { worktree ?=> … }` opens a working tree, or a linked
        worktree, and lends a `Git.Worktree.Handle`, which is both a `Git.Repo.Handle` — every
        repository operation applies without a hop — and a galilei `Directory.Handle`, so paths
        formed from it (`worktree / "src" / "main.scala"`) are typed to this handle alone,
        cannot escape it, cannot express `..`, and may be read and written with galilei's
        operations under the same grants. A bare repository raises `Git.Error(NoWorktree)`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import gitBackends.commandlineGit
        import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
        import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
        import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
        import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

        directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
          val head = worktree.head()
          (worktree / "notes.txt").overwrite(t"Remember the milk")
          worktree.add(worktree / "notes.txt")
          worktree.commit(t"Add notes", head)
          worktree.status().value   // Nil: nothing left to commit
        ```

        `Git.Worktree.Entry` is a linked worktree as `worktrees()` lists it, and the reading
        the worktree writes on a repository handle take.
    refer octogenarian.Git.Repo
    refer galilei.Directory
    entry .Entry   one linked worktree: its path, what is checked out, and whether it is locked or prunable
      detail
          What `worktrees()` on a repository handle lists, wrapped in `Stale`; the reading
          `removeWorktree`, `lockWorktree`, `unlockWorktree` and `moveWorktree` take.
      entry #path   the worktree's root
      entry #head   the commit checked out, if any
      entry #branch   the branch checked out, if attached
      entry #bare   whether the entry is the bare repository itself
      entry #locked   the lock's reason, if the worktree is locked
      entry #prunable   why the worktree could be pruned, if it could
    entry .Handle   a lent worktree handle: the index and working-file operations, over every repository operation
      detail
          Everything a `Git.Repo.Handle` offers, plus the operations which need an index and
          working files: staging (`.add`, `.addAll`, `.rm`, `.mv`, `.unstage`, `.restore`,
          `.clean`), committing (`.commit`, `.amend`), moving HEAD (`.reset`, `.checkout`,
          `.switch`), integrating (`.merge`, `.cherryPick`, `.revert`, `.rebase`, `.pull`),
          driving an operation in progress (`.continue`, `.skip`, `.abandon`, `.quit`), the
          stash (`.stash`, `.popStash`, `.applyStash`, `.dropStash`), and the reads which look
          at the working files (`.status`, `.diff`, `.diffStaged`, `.diffAgainst`, `.tracked`,
          `.conflicts`, `.currentBranch`, `.stashes`).

          Every operation which moves HEAD takes a `Stale[Git.Head]`, the reading from
          `head()`, and raises `Moved(HEAD, expected, actual)` if HEAD is elsewhere. Paths are
          `Path on worktree.Plane`, formed from the handle itself.

          A conflict is not an error: `merge`, `cherryPick`, `revert`, `rebase` and the stash
          applications return a `Stopped(operation)` outcome naming the paths in conflict; once
          they are resolved and added, `continue()` finishes the operation.
      entry .status   the state of every changed path, as a `Stale[List[Git.PathStatus[worktree.Plane]]]`
          Untracked files are included unless `untracked = false`; ignored ones only with
          `ignored = true`.
      entry .diff   the working files against the index, as a `Stale[List[FileDiff]]`
      entry .diffStaged   the index against HEAD, as a `Stale[List[FileDiff]]`
      entry .diffAgainst   the working files against a revision, as a `Stale[List[FileDiff]]`
      entry .diffBetween   the trees of two commits against each other, as a plain `List[FileDiff]`
          Keyed by hashes, so not `Stale`.
      entry .tracked   every path the index tracks, on the handle's plane, as a `Stale` list
      entry .conflicts   every path in conflict, on the handle's plane, as a `Stale` list
      entry .currentBranch   the branch checked out, or `Unset` when HEAD is detached, as a `Stale`
      entry .stashes   the stash's entries, newest first, as a `Stale[List[Git.Stash.Entry]]`
      entry .add   stages paths
          Takes any number of `Path on worktree.Plane`, formed as `worktree / "name"`.
      entry .addAll   stages every change, as `git add --all`
      entry .rm   removes paths from the index and, unless `cached`, from the working tree
          `rm(paths*)(cached, force)`: `force` removes a path with local changes.
      entry .mv   renames a path in the index and the working tree
      entry .unstage   removes paths from the index, leaving the working files as they are
      entry .restore   restores paths in the working tree (or, with `staged`, the index) from a revision
          `restore(paths*)(source, staged)`: from the index unless `source` is given.
      entry .clean   deletes untracked files, and directories or ignored files if asked, returning the paths removed
      entry .commit   commits the index, conditional on a reading of HEAD, returning the new commit's hash
          `commit(message, head, author, signer, allowEmpty)`: the tree is written, the commit
          object assembled here — the configured identity as committer (raising
          `NoSuchConfig` if `user.name` or `user.email` is unset), and as author too unless
          `author` is given — signed by the `Git.Signer` if one is given, stored, and HEAD moved by
          compare-and-swap. A tree unchanged from the parent's raises `NothingToCommit` unless
          `allowEmpty`. The object is assembled rather than made by `git commit`, so signing
          never leaves the process and the same code serves both backends; hooks are not run
          and `commit.gpgsign` is not consulted.
      entry .amend   rewrites the tip commit with a new message or signature, keeping its parents and author
          `amend(message, head, signer)`: the tree is the index as it stands now. Raises
          `NoSuchRef(HEAD)` on an unborn branch.
      entry .reset   moves HEAD to a revision under a `ResetMode`, conditional on a reading of HEAD
          `Soft` moves HEAD alone; `Mixed` resets the index too; `Hard` resets the working
          files as well; `Merge` and `Keep` are git's safer variants. Returns the commit moved
          to; raises `NoSuchRef` if the revision does not resolve.
      entry .checkout   checks a revision out, detaching HEAD, conditional on a reading of HEAD; returns the new reading
      entry .switch   switches to a branch, creating it at HEAD first if `create`, conditional on a reading of HEAD
          Returns the new `Stale[Git.Head]`. Raises `Dirty(paths)` if local changes would be
          overwritten, and `RefExists` if `create` names an existing branch.
      entry .merge   merges a revision into HEAD, conditional on a reading of HEAD, under a fast-forward policy
          `merge(revision, head, ff, message, sign)`: `FastForward.Auto` fast-forwards when it
          can, `Only` refuses anything else (`NotFastForward`), `Never` always makes a merge
          commit. The outcome is `FastForwarded`, `Merged`, `UpToDate` or `Stopped(operation)`
          on a conflict.
      entry .cherryPick   applies commits onto HEAD, conditional on a reading of HEAD; with `noCommit`, only to the index
      entry .revert   applies the inverse of commits onto HEAD, conditional on a reading of HEAD
      entry .rebase   rebases HEAD onto a revision, conditional on a reading of HEAD, with options
          `rebase(onto, head, upstream, options)`: `upstream` bounds the commits replayed, as
          `git rebase --onto` takes it. `Stopped(operation)` on a conflict carries how many
          commits are done and remaining.
      entry .pull   fetches a branch from a remote and merges or rebases it, conditional on a reading of HEAD
          `pull(remote, head, branch, mode, activity)`: the current branch unless `branch` is
          given (raising `Detached` if HEAD is on none); `Git.Pull.Mode.Merge` unless `mode`
          says otherwise. Needs an `Internet`.
      entry .continue   finishes the operation in progress once its conflicts are resolved and added
          Raises `NoOperationInProgress` if there is none.
      entry .skip   passes over the current commit of the operation in progress
      entry .abandon   abandons the operation in progress, restoring HEAD, the index and the working files to where it began
          Git's `--abort`. Returns the restored `Stale[Git.Head]`.
      entry .quit   leaves the operation in progress without restoring anything, as git's `--quit`
      entry .stash   saves the uncommitted state as a stash entry, returning its commit, or `Unset` if there was nothing
          `stash(message, includeUntracked, keepIndex)`.
      entry .popStash   applies a stash entry and drops it, conditional on the entry still being as listed
          Raises `NoSuchStash` if the entry has gone or changed. With `index = true` the index
          is restored too. A conflict is a `Stopped` outcome.
      entry .applyStash   applies a stash entry without dropping it, conditional on the entry still being as listed
      entry .dropStash   deletes a stash entry, conditional on the entry still being as listed
      entry #Under   the platform the worktree's paths are under: `Linux`
    entry .Openable   how a path opens as a `Git.Worktree`: locates the repository and its tree, and lends a `Handle`
      detail
          The `aperture.Openable` behind `path.open[Git.Worktree](mode, flags) { worktree ?=>
          … }`. It locates the repository (raising `NoRepository`), requires a working tree
          (raising `NoWorktree`), registers both the common directory and the tree when the
          mode is `Exclusive`, and lends a `Git.Worktree.Handle` with the mode's grants.
      entry #open   opens the path, lending a handle with the mode's grants to the block
      entry #Self   the path type opened
      entry #Form   the form opened in: `Git.Worktree`
      entry #Operand   the flag type: `Git.OpenFlag`
      entry #Result   what is lent: a `Git.Worktree.Handle`
    entry .openable   the given which lets any `Path on Linux` open as `Git.Worktree`
        In implicit scope; it needs a `Git.Backend^` (import one from `gitBackends`) and a
        `Tactic[Git.Error]`.
  entry .init   creates a repository at a path, with an initial branch, bare or not
      `Git.init(target, initialBranch, bare)` takes any path type. The initial branch is git's
      default (which depends on its configuration) unless given, so a program which will rely
      on `main` should say so. Needs a `Git.Backend^` and a `Tactic[Git.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
      Git.init(directory, initialBranch = Git.Branch(t"main"))
      Git.init(directory / "mirror.git", bare = true)
      ```
  entry .clone   clones a repository from a path, a URL or an `SshUrl` to a path
      `Git.clone(source, target, branch, depth, bare, recursive, activity)`: the source is a
      local path (any path type), a URL (any URL type, or text), or an `SshUrl`; `branch`
      checks out a branch other than the remote's HEAD; `depth` makes a shallow clone;
      `recursive` clones submodules (command-line backend only). Needs an `Internet`, a
      `Git.Backend^` and a `Tactic[Git.Error]`; progress is reported through `activity` if one
      is passed. Under the native backend a remote URL needs a `Transport` the backend was
      built with (`Native(List(SmartHttp()))`), else `Unsupported`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
      val target = directory / "clone"
      Git.clone(directory, target)

      target.open[Git.Worktree](): cloned ?=>
        cloned.remotes().value.map(_.name)   // List(t"origin")
      ```
  entry .cloneCommit   clones exactly one commit: a shallow fetch of it into a fresh repository, checked out
      `Git.cloneCommit(source, target, commit, activity)` initialises a repository at `target`,
      fetches the one commit at depth 1 and checks it out, detached. The server must allow
      fetching by hash (`uploadpack.allowReachableSHA1InWant`, or protocol version 2). Needs an
      `Internet`, a `Git.Backend^` and a `Tactic[Git.Error]`.

entry .Commit   a commit as stored: its hash, tree, parents, author, committer, signature and message
  detail
      What `log()` lists and `commitAt(hash)` reads: the commit's own hash, the tree it
      snapshots, its parents (none for a root commit, two for a merge), who wrote it and who
      committed it (each a `Git.Signature`, with a timestamp), the armoured signature block if
      it carries a `gpgsig` header, and its message without the trailing newline git appends.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

      directory.open[Git.Repo](): repo ?=>
        repo.log(limit = 1).value.map: commit =>
          t"${commit.commit} by ${commit.author.identity.name}: ${commit.message}"
      ```
  refer octogenarian.Git.Signature
  entry #commit   the commit's own hash
  entry #tree   the hash of the tree it snapshots
  entry #parents   its parents, oldest history first: none for a root commit, two for a merge
  entry #author   who wrote it and when
  entry #committer   who committed it and when
  entry #signature   the armoured signature from its `gpgsig` header, if it is signed
  entry #message   the message, without the trailing newline git stores

entry .ChangeKind   how a file changed: `Added`, `Modified`, `Deleted`, `Renamed`, `Copied` or `TypeChanged`
    Carried by each `FileDiff`. `Patch.parse` infers it from the patch headers: `/dev/null` on
    one side, `rename from`, `copy from`, `new file mode`, `deleted file mode`.

entry .FastForward   whether a merge may, must or must not fast-forward: `Auto`, `Only` or `Never`
    The policy `merge` takes, so that whether a merge commit is created is stated rather than
    inherited from configuration. `Auto` fast-forwards when it can; `Only` refuses anything
    else, raising `NotFastForward`; `Never` always makes a merge commit.

entry .FileDiff   the change to one file: its paths on both sides, the kind of change, and its hunks
  detail
      What `diff()`, `diffStaged()`, `diffAgainst(revision)` and `diffBetween(left, right)`
      report, one per file, and what `Patch.parse` reads from patch text. A binary diff or a
      mode-only change has no hunks. `Patch.asDiff` flattens the hunks into one dissonance
      `Diff[Text]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

      directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
        (worktree / "notes.txt").overwrite(t"Remember the milk and the eggs")
        worktree.diff().value.map: file =>
          t"${file.newPath.or(t"?")}: ${file.changeKind}, ${file.hunks.size} hunks"
      ```
  refer octogenarian.Hunk
  refer octogenarian.Patch
  entry #oldPath   the path before the change, `Unset` for an added file
  entry #newPath   the path after the change, `Unset` for a deleted file
  entry #changeKind   how the file changed
  entry #hunks   the hunks, in order; empty for a binary or mode-only change

entry .Hunk   one hunk of a file's diff: its ranges, its section heading, and its edits
  detail
      The `@@ -oldStart,oldLines +newStart,newLines @@ section` header of a unified diff,
      with its edits as dissonance `Edit[Text]`s — `Ins`, `Del` and `Par` — each carrying its
      text, so a hunk is `Retained` and supports the value-demanding operations such as
      `redraft`.
  entry #oldStart   the first line of the hunk on the old side
  entry #oldLines   how many old lines the hunk spans
  entry #newStart   the first line of the hunk on the new side
  entry #newLines   how many new lines the hunk spans
  entry #section   the heading after the `@@`: the enclosing function or class, if git found one
  entry #edits   the insertions, deletions and unchanged lines, in order

entry .Notes   the plane of git notes: a path rooted at a commit hash, descending through a namespace
  detail
      A git note is text attached to a commit without altering it, in a namespace under
      `refs/notes/`. Modelling the note's address as a serpentine path — the commit hash is
      the root (`Git.Hash` is a `Root`), and the namespace segments are the descent — lets
      `hash / t"attestations"` build a `NoteRef` with the path operator itself, and the
      `NoteRef` extensions `target`, `namespace` and `content` read it back.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val hash = Git.Hash(t"5c1d2b7e3a4f60918273645566778899aabbccdd")
      val ref: NoteRef = hash / t"attestations" / t"build"
      ref.encode              // t"5c1d…ccdd/attestations/build"
      ref.target == hash
      ref.namespace.encode    // t"refs/notes/attestations/build"
      ```
  refer octogenarian.NoteRef
  refer octogenarian.content
  entry .Plane   the plane type of notes paths: `Notes` itself
  entry .filesystem   the notes plane as a `Filesystem`: `/`-separated, `@` and `..` for self and parent
  entry .admissible   the given which admits any text as a namespace segment
      Validation is deferred to `namespace`, which checks each segment against git's
      reference rules when the full ref is needed.
  entry .radical   how a commit hash encodes as the root of a notes path: `<hash>/`
  entry .eradicable   how a commit hash is read back from the root of a notes path
      Decodes forty hexadecimal digits followed by `/`, raising `Path.Error(InvalidRoot)` for
      anything else; needs a `Tactic[Path.Error]`.

entry .NoteRef   the address of a note: `Path on Notes under Git.Hash`, a commit hash with a namespace beneath it
    A `NoteRef` is built as `hash / t"namespace"` (and may descend further: `hash / t"ci" /
    t"attest"`). Its root is the commit the note is attached to (`target`), its descent is
    the namespace under `refs/notes/` (`namespace`), and `content[value]` reads the note
    through the repository handle in scope.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import octogenarian.content
    import gitBackends.commandlineGit
    import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]

    directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
      val hash = worktree.head().value.commit.or(Git.Hash.zero)
      val ref = Git.Refs.notes(t"greeting")
      worktree.addNote(hash, t"hello", worktree.ref(ref), ref = ref)
      (hash / t"greeting").content[Text].value   // t"hello"
    ```

entry .target   the commit a `NoteRef` is attached to: its root hash
    An extension on `NoteRef`, reading the forty digits of the path's root back as a
    `Git.Hash`.

entry .namespace   the fully-qualified notes ref a `NoteRef` names: `refs/notes/<segments>`
    An extension on `NoteRef`. Each segment of the descent is checked against git's
    reference rules here, raising `Git.RefError` for one git would reject, since `/` admits
    any text when the path is built.

entry .content   the note a `NoteRef` addresses, decoded, read through the repository handle in scope
    An extension on `NoteRef`: `(hash / t"ns").content[Text]` is the note's body as a
    `Stale[Text]`, or decoded as any `value is Decodable in Text`. It needs a `Git.Repo.Handle
    & Granting[Grant.Read]` in scope — the handle an `open` block lends — and raises
    `Git.Error(NoteNotFound)` if no note is attached in that namespace. The result is
    `Stale`, since a note is reached through a ref another process may move. It is named
    `content` rather than `read`, since a `NoteRef` is a path and paths are readable;
    import it by name (`import octogenarian.content`) so that it wins on specificity.

entry .Patch   reads patch text as `FileDiff`s, and flattens a file's hunks into one `Diff`
  detail
      `Patch.parse` reads the output of `git diff -p --no-color` — from a file, a mail, or
      anywhere else — as one `FileDiff` per `diff --git` header, handling modified, added,
      deleted, renamed and copied files; a binary diff or a mode-only change gives a
      `FileDiff` with no hunks. It is the same parser the command-line backend uses on git's
      own output. `Patch(files)` is a parsed patch as a value.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val lines = List
        ( t"diff --git a/notes.txt b/notes.txt",
          t"--- a/notes.txt",
          t"+++ b/notes.txt",
          t"@@ -1 +1 @@",
          t"-Remember the milk",
          t"+Remember the milk and the eggs" )

      val files = Patch.parse(lines)
      files.head.let(_.changeKind)   // ChangeKind.Modified
      files.head.let(Patch.asDiff(_).edits.size)   // 2
      ```
  refer octogenarian.FileDiff
  entry .parse   the `FileDiff`s in lines of unified diff text
  entry .asDiff   a file's hunks flattened into one dissonance `Diff[Text] & Retained`
      Assembled from the hunks' `Retained` edits, so the diff carries every line's text and
      supports `redraft` and the other value-demanding operations.
  entry #files   the patch's files, in order

entry .ReflogEntry   one line of a reflog: where a ref pointed, when, and why it moved
  detail
      What `reflog()` lists, newest first: the hash the ref held after the move, the selector
      (`HEAD@{0}`), the time of the move in seconds since the epoch, and the message the
      operation recorded (`commit: Add notes`, `branch: Created from …`, `reset: moving to
      main`).
  entry #hash   the hash the ref held after the move
  entry #selector   the entry's selector, `HEAD@{0}`
  entry #timestamp   when the ref moved, in seconds since the epoch
  entry #message   why it moved, as the operation recorded it

entry .Remote   a remote by its name, fetch URL and push URL
  detail
      What `remotes()` lists and `addRemote` returns, read from the repository's own
      configuration (`remote.<name>.url` and `remote.<name>.pushurl`). The URL is text: a
      local path, an HTTP(S) URL or an SSH address, as git accepts them.
  entry #name   the remote's name, `origin`
  entry #fetchUrl   the URL fetched from
  entry #pushUrl   the URL pushed to, if it differs from `#fetchUrl`

entry .ResetMode   what `reset` moves besides HEAD: `Soft`, `Mixed`, `Hard`, `Merge` or `Keep`
  detail
      `Soft` moves HEAD alone, leaving the index and working files as they are; `Mixed` resets
      the index too; `Hard` resets the working files as well, discarding local changes;
      `Merge` and `Keep` are git's variants which refuse to discard uncommitted changes.
      `#flag` is the command-line spelling.
  entry #flag   the flag git knows the mode by: `--soft`, `--mixed`, `--hard`, `--merge`, `--keep`

entry .SshUrl   a repository's address over SSH: an optional user, a checked hostname and a path
  detail
      `SshUrl(user, hostname, path)` is the one source type `Git.clone` and `Git.cloneCommit`
      accept besides paths and URLs; `#text` renders it as the address git is given. The
      hostname is a urticose `Hostname`, so it has been checked. For the address as text in
      either of git's SSH forms, `SshAddress.parse` is the reader the transports use.
  entry #user   the user to connect as, if any
  entry #hostname   the host
  entry #path   the repository's path on the host
  entry #text   the address as git takes it

entry .Commandline   the backend which runs the `git` command, as `gitBackends.commandlineGit`
  detail
      A `Git.Backend` whose every operation is one or a few invocations of git's plumbing;
      porcelain verbs run the porcelain command and read its outcome. Every child runs in the
      `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in scope, under `LC_ALL=C` so that its messages are
      the English ones the reason table knows, and with `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` so that a
      credential prompt fails rather than hangs. Each invocation is logged as a
      `Commandline.Event`, so a `Commandline.Event is Loggable` must be in scope
      (`logging.silentLogging` discards them).

      It is the choice when git's own behaviour is wanted in full — hooks (though not on
      `commit`, which assembles the object itself), filters, credential helpers, submodules —
      and it brings git's own transports, so a remote URL needs nothing more.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging

      val backend: Git.Backend^ = Commandline(Commandline.Command(t"/usr/bin/git"))
      ```

      `.reason` is the table which reads git's standard error as a `Git.Error.Reason`;
      anything unrecognised is `Unexpected`, with the message kept as the error's `detail`,
      so nothing is discarded. A failure to run `git` at all is `CannotExecuteGit`.
  refer octogenarian.gitBackends
  refer octogenarian.gitCommands
  refer octogenarian.Git.Backend
  entry .Command   the `git` executable to run, by its path
    detail
        `gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand` finds it on the search path; a program which bundles
        its own git names it explicitly, `Commandline.Command(t"/opt/git/bin/git")`.
    entry #path   the executable's path, as text
  entry .Event   what the backend logs: each `git` invocation, as a guillotine `Exec.Event`
    detail
        `Exec(event)` wraps a `guillotine.Exec.Event`, tagged `Log.Process`; `.execEvent`
        transcribes guillotine's events into it, so a `Commandline.Event is Loggable` in scope
        is all the backend needs. `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
    entry .execEvent   transcribes a guillotine `Exec.Event` into a `Commandline.Event`
    entry .communicable   renders an event as _the git operation did not execute: `reason`_
  entry .Outcome   what one `git` invocation produced: its exit status, standard output and standard error
    entry #exit   the exit status
    entry #stdout   standard output, as text
    entry #stderr   standard error, as text
    entry #ok   whether the exit status was zero
    entry #lines   standard output as non-empty lines
  entry .reason   reads git's standard error as a `Git.Error.Reason`
      Matches git's messages — stable enough to match on — for `NoRepository`, `Moved`,
      `RefExists`, `Locked`, `NoSuchRef`, `Unmerged`, `NoSuchObject`, `NothingToCommit`,
      `Detached`, `Dirty`, `NotFastForward`, `LeaseFailed`, `OutsideWorktree`, `Untracked`,
      `NoOperationInProgress`, `NoSuchRemote`, `RemoteExists`, `Unauthorized`, `Unreachable`,
      `RemoteRejected`, `InvalidConfig`, `NoteExists`, `BadSignature`, `SigningFailed`,
      `NoSuchWorktree` and `Conflict`; anything else is `Unexpected`.

entry .Gpg   signing and verifying with OpenPGP keys through `gpg`, as git does
  detail
      `Gpg.Signer()` makes a detached, armoured signature over a commit's or tag's payload
      with the default key, or with `key` (a key id, fingerprint or user id);
      `Gpg.Verifier()` checks one against the keyring, reading `GOODSIG`, `BADSIG` and the
      rest from gpg's machine-readable status lines. Both run `gpg` in the `WorkingDirectory`
      and `Environment` in scope, logging as `Exec.Event`s, and report a failure to run it as
      `Git.Error(SigningFailed)`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
      given Git.Verifier = Gpg.Verifier()

      directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
        val hash = worktree.commit(t"Signed", worktree.head(), signer = Gpg.Signer(), allowEmpty = true)
        worktree.verifyCommit(hash)   // Git.Verification.Good(userId)
      ```
  refer octogenarian.Git.Signer
  refer octogenarian.SshKeygen
  entry .Signer   signs with the default OpenPGP key, or with `key`, through `gpg -bsa`
    entry #sign   the armoured signature over a payload; raises `Git.Error(SigningFailed)` if gpg fails
  entry .Verifier   verifies against the keyring through `gpg --verify`
    entry #verify   `Good(userId)` for a `GOODSIG` line, `Bad` for `BADSIG`, otherwise `Unknown`

entry .SmartHttp   remote repositories over HTTP(S) by git's smart protocol, as `gitTransports.smartHttp`
  detail
      A `Transport` for the native backend: the capability advertisement is read from
      `info/refs?service=…`, and each command is a `POST` to the service, with protocol
      version 2 requested in the `Git-Protocol` header. Credentials in the URL's userinfo
      (`https://user:token@host/…`) are sent as Basic authentication. It speaks through
      whichever `Http.Backend` is in scope, so `SmartHttp()` needs one
      (`httpBackends.javaNetHttp`, say), and `Native(List(SmartHttp()))` is a backend which can
      clone, fetch and push over HTTP(S).

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import internetAccess.online
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Git.Backend = Native(List(SmartHttp()))

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/soundness".as[Path on Linux]
      Git.clone(t"https://github.com/propensive/soundness", directory, depth = 1)
      ```

      The server's answers become `Git.Error`s: `401` and `403` are `Unauthorized`, `404` is
      `NoRepository`, a connection failure is `Unreachable`, and any other failure
      `Unexpected` with the status code as detail.
  refer octogenarian.gitTransports
  refer octogenarian.Native
  entry .Channel   one repository's HTTP endpoint: the advertisement and each service exchange
    detail
        The `Channel` the protocol drives: `#advertise` fetches `info/refs` and skips the
        `# service=…` line the HTTP form adds in front, and `#exchange` posts a request to the
        service and returns its packet-line reply. Nothing is kept between exchanges.
    entry #advertise   the capability advertisement of a service, as a packet-line reader
    entry #exchange   posts a request to a service and returns its reply, as a packet-line reader
    entry #close   nothing to close: each exchange is its own request
  entry #accepts   whether a URL begins with `http://` or `https://`
  entry #connect   a `Peer` over the URL, raising `Git.Error` for a server which refuses or cannot be reached

entry .SshCommand   remote repositories through a spawned `ssh`, as `gitTransports.sshCommand`
  detail
      A `Transport` for the native backend which reaches a server as git itself does: one `ssh`
      process per service, running `git-upload-pack` or `git-receive-pack` on the server with
      protocol version 2 requested through the `GIT_PROTOCOL` variable (sent with `SendEnv`,
      which the server honours where its `AcceptEnv` allows). Both `ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path`
      and the `scp`-like `[user@]host:path` forms are understood, through `SshAddress.parse`.
      The user's own `ssh` configuration, keys and agent apply, as they would at a shell.

      `SshCommand()` runs in the `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in scope, logging each
      process as an `Exec.Event`. A fetch costs two connections — one for `ls-refs` and one for
      `fetch` — since nothing is kept between exchanges.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Git.Backend = Native(List(SshCommand()))

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/soundness".as[Path on Linux]
      Git.clone(t"git@github.com:propensive/soundness.git", directory, depth = 1)
      ```
  refer octogenarian.gitTransports
  refer octogenarian.SshAddress
  refer octogenarian.SshNative
  entry .Channel   one repository reached over a spawned `ssh`: a process per exchange
    detail
        The `Channel` the protocol drives. The server begins every connection by advertising,
        which `#advertise` returns and `#exchange` reads past before returning the reply to
        the request it wrote. A failure to start `ssh`, or a reply cut short, is
        `Git.Error(Unreachable(host))`.
    entry #advertise   starts a service and returns its advertisement, as a packet-line reader
    entry #exchange   starts a service, writes a request to it and returns its reply, past the advertisement
    entry #close   nothing to close: each exchange is its own process
  entry #accepts   whether a URL is an SSH address, in either form
  entry #connect   a `Peer` over the address, raising `Git.Error(Unsupported)` for a URL which is not one

entry .SshKeygen   signing and verifying with OpenSSH keys through `ssh-keygen -Y`, as `gpg.format = ssh` has git do
  detail
      `SshKeygen.Signer(key)` signs a commit's or tag's payload in the `git` namespace with
      the private key at a path; `SshKeygen.Verifier(allowedSigners)` finds the principal a
      signature's key speaks for in an allowed-signers file (the one git's
      `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` names, each line `principal key-type key`) and verifies
      against it. Both run `ssh-keygen` in the `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in scope,
      logging as `Exec.Event`s, and report a failure to run it as `Git.Error(SigningFailed)`.
      A commit or tag signed this way is one `git log --show-signature` and `git tag -v`
      accept.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/repo".as[Path on Linux]
      val key: Path on Linux = t"/home/jon/.ssh/id_ed25519".as[Path on Linux]
      val allowed: Path on Linux = t"/home/jon/.ssh/allowed_signers".as[Path on Linux]
      given Git.Verifier = SshKeygen.Verifier(allowed)

      directory.open[Git.Worktree](Read & Write): worktree ?=>
        val hash = worktree.commit(t"Signed", worktree.head(), signer = SshKeygen.Signer(key), allowEmpty = true)
        worktree.verifyCommit(hash)   // Git.Verification.Good(principal)
      ```

      A `tag.gpgsign` or `commit.gpgsign` setting in git's configuration is not consulted:
      signing happens only when a `Git.Signer` is passed, and never leaves the process.
  refer octogenarian.Git.Signer
  refer octogenarian.Git.Verifier
  refer octogenarian.Gpg
  entry .Signer   signs with the private key at a path, through `ssh-keygen -Y sign -n git`
    entry #sign   the armoured signature over a payload; raises `Git.Error(SigningFailed)` if ssh-keygen fails
  entry .Verifier   verifies against the principals and keys in an allowed-signers file
    entry #verify   `Good(principal)` if the key is allowed and the signature holds; `Unknown` if no principal matches; `Bad` otherwise

entry .SshNative   remote repositories over the native SSH client, with no `ssh` binary, as `gitTransports.soundnessSsh`
  detail
      A `Transport` for the native backend which connects through susurration, the SSH client
      written in Scala: one SSH connection per peer, with a channel per exchange, each
      running `git-upload-pack` or `git-receive-pack` with `GIT_PROTOCOL=version=2`. The host
      key is checked against the `Ssh.HostKeys` policy in scope, the user's identities come
      from `Ssh.Credentials` (the address's user, else the credentials' user for the host, else
      `git`), and the socket from the `Socket.Backend`; so `SshNative()` takes those, with
      `Ssh.Provider`, `Ssh.Algorithms`, the TCP options, a SHA-256 `Hash` and `Diagnostics`.
      Both address forms `SshCommand` accepts are accepted here.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import gitTransports.soundnessSsh
      import internetAccess.online
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Git.Backend = Native(List(soundnessSsh))

      val directory: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/soundness".as[Path on Linux]
      Git.clone(t"ssh://git@github.com/propensive/soundness.git", directory, depth = 1)
      ```

      A connection, authentication or SSH failure is `Git.Error(Unreachable(host))`, with the
      failure's own message as detail; a port outside `1`–`65535` is `Unsupported`.
  refer octogenarian.gitTransports
  refer octogenarian.SshCommand
  refer susurration.Ssh
  entry .Channel   one SSH connection to a repository, with a channel per exchange
    detail
        The `Channel` the protocol drives. A previous exchange's SSH channel is closed before
        the next opens, since the transport is driven by one thread; the connection itself
        stays open until `#close`.
    entry #advertise   opens a channel for a service and returns its advertisement, as a packet-line reader
    entry #exchange   opens a channel for a service, writes a request and returns its reply, past the advertisement
    entry #close   closes the current channel, the session and the socket
  entry #accepts   whether a URL is an SSH address, in either form
  entry #connect   connects, authenticates and returns a `Peer`; raises `Git.Error(Unreachable)` on failure

entry .gitCommands   where the `git` executable is, each a given to import by name
  detail
      The command-line backend needs a `Commandline.Command` naming the `git` to run. Importing
      `searchpathGitCommand` finds it on the search path; a program which bundles its own git
      binds `given Commandline.Command = Commandline.Command(t"/opt/git/bin/git")` instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      ```
  entry .searchpathGitCommand   the `git` found on the search path, by `which git`
      Resolved when the given is first used, in the `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in
      scope, and logged as a `Commandline.Event`. It needs a `Tactic[Exec.Error]`,
      `Tactic[Path.Error]`, `Tactic[Io.Error]` and `Tactic[Name.Error]` in scope — which
      `strategies.throwUnsafely` covers — and a `Path on Linux` instantiable from text.

entry .gitBackends   the two implementations of `Git.Backend`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which backend carries out the operations is decided by the `Git.Backend^` given in
      scope, and the choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings neither in, and one
      is imported by name. Both are permanent; the same code runs under either.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.commandlineGit
      import gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging
      ```

      `commandlineGit` runs the `git` command and so has git's every behaviour, transport and
      credential helper. `soundnessGit` is `Native()`, which reads and writes the repository's
      files itself and needs no `git`; a remote repository then needs a `Transport`, so a
      program which clones, fetches or pushes over the network under the native backend binds
      its own `given Git.Backend = Native(List(SmartHttp(), SshCommand()))` with the
      `gitTransports` givens in scope, rather than importing `soundnessGit`.
  refer octogenarian.Git.Backend
  refer octogenarian.Commandline
  refer octogenarian.Native
  entry .commandlineGit   the backend which runs the `git` command
      A `Git.Backend^{loggable, caps.any}` built as `Commandline(command)` from the
      `Commandline.Command`, `WorkingDirectory`, `Environment` and `Commandline.Event is
      Loggable` in scope: so import `gitCommands.searchpathGitCommand` (or bind a command),
      `workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory`, `environments.javaBaseEnvironment` and a
      logging policy alongside it.

  entry .soundnessGit   the native backend: reads and writes the repository's files itself, with no `git` binary
      `Native()` with no transports, so it reaches repositories on the same filesystem by path
      and raises `Git.Error(Unsupported(the transport for <url>))` for any other URL; a
      program which fetches from or pushes to a remote over HTTP or SSH binds its own
      `given Git.Backend = Native(List(SmartHttp(), SshCommand()))` instead. Imported by name,
      since `import soundness.*` does not import givens; `gitBackends.commandlineGit` is the
      alternative which runs the `git` command.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.soundnessGit
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory
      import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem

      val directory = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "git" / Uuid().show
      directory.create[Directory]()
      Git.init(directory, initialBranch = Git.Branch(t"main"))
      directory.open[Git.Repo]() { repo ?=> repo.head().value }
      ```

entry .gitTransports   the ways the native backend reaches a remote repository, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Transport` is how `Native` reaches a URL: `SmartHttp` over HTTP(S), `SshCommand`
      through a spawned `ssh`, and `SshNative` through the native SSH client. The givens here
      make each from the context it needs, so that `Native(List(smartHttp, sshCommand))` can be
      written with the transports named rather than constructed. The command-line backend
      needs none of them.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import gitTransports.{smartHttp, sshCommand}
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
      import logging.silentLogging

      given Git.Backend = Native(List(smartHttp, sshCommand))
      ```
  refer octogenarian.SmartHttp
  refer octogenarian.SshCommand
  refer octogenarian.SshNative
  entry .smartHttp   `SmartHttp()` over the `Http.Backend` in scope
      Needs an `Http.Backend`, such as `httpBackends.javaNetHttp`.
  entry .sshCommand   `SshCommand()` in the `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in scope
      A `Transport^{loggable, caps.any}`: it keeps the `Exec.Event is Loggable` it was built
      from, so a logging policy must be in scope.
  entry .soundnessSsh   `SshNative()` from the susurration context in scope
      Needs a `Socket.Backend`, the TCP `Socket.Option`s, an `Ssh.Provider`, `Ssh.Algorithms`,
      `Ssh.HostKeys`, `Ssh.Credentials`, a SHA-256 `Hash` and `Diagnostics`, and `Online` as
      evidence that the network may be used (demanded, as telekinesis's HTTP clients demand
      it, but not kept: the transport itself is pure).

entry .Native   the `Git.Backend` which reads and writes a repository's files itself, with no `git` binary
  detail
      `Native` is the pure-Scala implementation of `Git.Backend`: every operation a `Git.Repo`
      or `Git.Worktree` handle performs is carried out by reading and writing the repository's
      own files, in git's own formats, so a repository it touches is always one `git` itself
      understands, and an operation one side stops on a conflict the other can continue.
      `gitBackends.soundnessGit` is the given built from `Native()`, chosen by importing it by
      name; `gitBackends.commandlineGit` is the alternative which runs the `git` command.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import gitBackends.soundnessGit
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.javaBaseTemporaryDirectory
      import filesystemOptions.createNonexistentParents
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem

      val directory = temporaryDirectory[Path on Linux] / "git" / Uuid().show
      directory.create[Directory]()
      Git.init(directory, initialBranch = Git.Branch(t"main"))

      directory.open[Git.Worktree](): worktree ?=>
        worktree.head().value   // Git.Head.Attached(Git.Branch(t"main"), Unset)
      ```

      What it reads and writes:

      - **Objects**: loose objects under `objects/`, packfiles under `objects/pack/` with their
        `.idx` indexes (offset and reference deltas resolved), and the stores named in
        `objects/info/alternates`. Objects it writes are always loose, written to a temporary
        name and moved into place, so a concurrent reader never sees a partial object; a pack
        received by a fetch or clone is held whole in memory and unpacked to loose objects.
      - **Refs**: loose refs, `packed-refs`, symbolic refs and reflogs. Every ref-moving write
        is a compare-and-swap under a `<ref>.lock` file taken with an exclusive create: a lock
        already held is `Git.Error(Locked)`, and a ref which no longer holds what the caller's
        `Stale` reading said is `Git.Error(Moved)`, reported under the name the caller used
        (`HEAD`), as git reports it.
      - **Configuration**: `/etc/gitconfig`, the XDG and `~/.gitconfig` files, the repository's
        `config` and, when `extensions.worktreeconfig` is set, `config.worktree`, in git's order,
        with `include.path` files spliced in. The committer identity comes from `user.name` and
        `user.email`, and `core.logallrefupdates` decides whether reflogs are written.
      - **The index and working tree**: the index file (version 2, with unknown extensions
        preserved), `.gitignore` files and `info/exclude`, status, add, remove, move, restore,
        clean, checkout and switch.
      - **Sequencing**: merge (with a diff3 three-way merge and conflict markers), cherry-pick,
        revert, rebase and the stash, with the same state files git uses (`MERGE_HEAD`,
        `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD`, `sequencer/`, `rebase-merge/`, `refs/stash`), so `git` can continue
        or abort an operation `Native` stopped, and the reverse.
      - **Notes** under any notes ref, **linked worktrees** (`worktrees/` registry), and
        **transfers**: fetch, push, clone and `remoteRefs`, against a repository on the same
        filesystem by path, or over the wire protocol through a `Transport`.

      A repository on the same filesystem is reached by its path, or a `file://`, `./` or `../`
      URL, as a `Peer.Local`, with no transport at all. Any other URL needs a `Transport` which
      accepts it, from the `transports` list the backend is constructed with: `SmartHttp()`
      (the `http` component) for `http://` and `https://`, and `SshCommand()` (the `ssh`
      component) or `SshNative()` (the `sshNative` component) for `ssh://` URLs and `scp`-style
      `host:path` addresses. `gitBackends.soundnessGit` is `Native()` with no transports, so it
      reaches local repositories only; a URL no transport accepts is
      `Git.Error(Unsupported(the transport for <url>))`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Local repositories only
        code
            given Git.Backend = Native()
      frame
        label Smart HTTP added
        code
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

            given Git.Backend = Native(List(SmartHttp()))
      frame
        label Smart HTTP and a spawned ssh
        code
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
            import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory
            import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
            import logging.silentLogging

            given Git.Backend = Native(List(SmartHttp(), SshCommand()))
      ##
      ```

      The first transport whose `accepts` is true for the URL is used, so order the list by
      preference. Whatever the transport, protocol version 2 is spoken for listing refs and
      fetching, and the version 0 `receive-pack` exchange for pushing, as git does.

      What it does not do, each reported as `Git.Error(Unsupported(…))`: cloning submodules
      recursively, signing a merge commit (signing commits and tags is independent of the
      backend, through a `Git.Signer`), `rebase --autosquash`, continuing an `am`-style
      rebase, skipping a merge, and `reset` in any mode but soft, mixed and hard. Hooks,
      filters and credential helpers are never run. A filesystem failure beneath any operation
      is reported as `Git.Error(Unexpected)` with the failure's own message as its detail.

      `Native` is available on the JVM (`native-jvm`, over galilei's `java.nio.file` backend)
      and on Scala Native (`native-native`, over the javalib filesystem). It is exported to the
      `soundness` umbrella, as are `Transport`, `Peer` and `SshAddress`.
  refer octogenarian.gitBackends
  refer octogenarian.Transport
  refer octogenarian.Peer
  refer octogenarian.SshAddress
  entry #locate   finds the repository a path belongs to: its git directory, common directory and worktree
      A directory with a `.git` directory is a worktree; one whose `.git` is a file naming a
      `gitdir:` is a linked worktree, whose own git directory names the shared one in
      `commondir`; a directory with `HEAD`, `objects` and `refs` is a git directory itself (a
      bare repository, or a `.git` whose parent is the worktree). Anything else, or a path
      which does not exist, is `Unset`. This is what `path.open[Git.Repo]` and
      `open[Git.Worktree]` consult.
  entry #init   creates a repository, with `HEAD` on the initial branch
      Makes `objects/`, `refs/heads/`, `refs/tags/`, `info/` and the rest, writes `HEAD` as a
      symbolic ref to the branch (`main` if none is named), a `config` with
      `core.repositoryformatversion`, `core.filemode`, `core.bare` and, for a non-bare
      repository, `core.logallrefupdates`, and the `description` file. A bare repository is
      made at the target itself, otherwise in its `.git`. Files already present are kept, so
      re-initialising is harmless. `Git.init` is the front door.
  entry #clone   fetches a repository into a fresh one and checks out its default branch
      Connects to the source (a local path, or a URL some transport accepts), asks for its
      symbolic `HEAD` to choose the branch when none is named, initialises the target, writes
      the `origin` remote with the usual fetch refspec (`+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*`,
      or `+refs/heads/*:refs/heads/*` for a bare clone), fetches, and for a non-bare clone
      creates the local branch with its upstream configured, checks the tree out and writes
      the index. A `depth` makes a shallow clone whose `shallow` file git honours. It needs
      `Internet` evidence, and cloning submodules recursively is
      `Git.Error(Unsupported(cloning submodules recursively))`. The `Git.Activity` it is given
      reports the `Receiving` and `Checkout` phases.
  entry #head   what `HEAD` is: attached to a branch, perhaps unborn, or detached at a hash
      A symbolic `HEAD` is `Git.Head.Attached` with the branch's tip, or `Unset` for an unborn
      branch; a direct one is `Git.Head.Detached`. A repository with no readable `HEAD` is
      `Git.Error(NoSuchRef(HEAD))`.
  entry #readRef   a ref's content, direct or symbolic, from the loose file or `packed-refs`
  entry #listRefs   every ref under a prefix, with its hash, in name order
      Loose refs shadow packed ones of the same name, as they do for git.
  entry #peel   follows annotated tags to the object they finally name
  entry #updateRef   compare-and-swap of a ref: moves or deletes it if it holds what was expected
      Takes `<ref>.lock` with an exclusive create (`Git.Error(Locked)` if another process
      holds it), compares the ref's current value with the `Git.Backend.Expectation`
      (`Git.Error(Moved)` if it differs), writes or deletes, and appends a reflog line with
      the message when `core.logallrefupdates` says so. A packed ref being updated is written
      loose. This is the primitive every ref-moving verb on a handle rests on.
  entry #updateSymbolicRef   points a symbolic ref (such as `HEAD`) at another ref
  entry #resolve   a revision expression to a hash, or `Unset` if nothing matches
      Understands full and unambiguous abbreviated hashes, `HEAD` and `@`, ref names tried in
      git's order (`refs/<name>`, `refs/tags/<name>`, `refs/heads/<name>`,
      `refs/remotes/<name>`, …), reflog entries as `<ref>@{n}`, and the `~n`, `^n`, `^{}`,
      `^{commit}`, `^{tree}` and `^{tag}` suffixes, which bind from the right.
  entry #revList   the commits reachable from one revision and not another, newest first
      Walks parents from `from`, stopping at `until` and at the `shallow` boundary, in
      descending commit-date order; `limit` caps the count and `paths` keeps only commits
      which change one of them. An unresolvable revision is `Git.Error(NoSuchRef)`.
  entry #reflog   the reflog of a ref (`HEAD` by default), newest first
  entry #mergeBase   the nearest common ancestor of two commits, or `Unset` if there is none
  entry #isAncestor   whether one commit is reachable from another
  entry #readObject   an object's kind and bytes, from the loose store, a pack or an alternate
      `Unset` when no store has it. Packs are re-listed when a hash is not found, since another
      process may have repacked.
  entry #readObjects   several objects at once, skipping any which are missing
  entry #writeObject   stores an object loose and returns its hash
      Hashed as git hashes it, over the `<kind> <size>\0` header and the bytes; an object
      already present is not rewritten.
  entry #diffTree   the file differences between two trees, as `FileDiff`s with hunks
  entry #status   the index and working-tree state of every path, as `git status --porcelain` reports it
      Compares `HEAD`'s tree, the index and the working files, honouring `.gitignore` and
      `info/exclude`; `ignored` and `untracked` say whether those files are listed. Index
      entries whose stat data still matches the file are trusted without hashing it, as git's
      racy-git rules allow.
  entry #add   stages paths, or with `all` every change under the worktree
      Writes blobs for the files and updates the index, honouring `.gitignore` for files not
      already staged; a path which matches no file and is not staged is
      `Git.Error(Untracked(path))`.
  entry #remove   removes paths from the index and, unless `cached`, from the working tree
      A path the index does not track is `Git.Error(Untracked(path))`, and a file whose staged
      or working content differs from `HEAD` is `Git.Error(Dirty)` without `force`.
  entry #move   renames a tracked path in the index and on disk
  entry #restore   restores paths from the index or from a source revision, to the index, the working files, or both
  entry #clean   deletes untracked files, and with `directories` untracked directories, returning what went
      Ignored files go only when `ignored` is set.
  entry #writeTree   writes the index as a tree object and returns its hash
      An index with unmerged entries is `Git.Error(Conflict(paths))`.
  entry #readTree   resets the index, and for `ResetMode.Hard` the working files, to `HEAD`
      `Soft` changes nothing; `Mixed` rewrites the index from `HEAD` with stat data left
      unverified, so the next status hashes each file; `Hard` also rewrites the working files,
      comparing against what is on disk rather than the index so a modified file is replaced.
      Any other mode is `Git.Error(Unsupported)`.
  entry #diffWorktree   the differences between the working files (or the index, with `staged`) and `HEAD` or a revision
  entry #tracked   every path in the index
  entry #conflicts   the paths with unmerged index entries
  entry #checkout   moves `HEAD` to a revision, detaching it, and updates the index and files
      A branch name is checked out attached, anything else detached. Paths modified in the
      index or working tree which the checkout would overwrite make it `Git.Error(Dirty)`.
  entry #switch   moves `HEAD` to a branch, creating it from the current commit with `create`
      Without `create`, a branch which does not exist is `Git.Error(NoSuchRef)`, and the
      switch is a checkout of the branch, attached. With it, the branch is created at the
      current commit by a compare-and-swap expecting it absent, so a branch which already
      exists is `Git.Error(Moved)`, and `HEAD` is pointed at it without touching the files.
  entry #merge   merges a revision into `HEAD`: fast-forward, a merge commit, or a stop on conflicts
      Honours the `FastForward` policy, writes conflict markers from a diff3 merge of the two
      sides against their merge base, and on conflicts leaves `MERGE_HEAD` and `MERGE_MSG`
      for `sequence` to continue or abort. Signing a merge commit natively is
      `Git.Error(Unsupported)`.
  entry #cherryPick   applies commits one by one onto `HEAD`, stopping on the first conflict
      Writes `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD` and a `sequencer/` todo list which keeps the current item,
      exactly as git does, so either side can continue. With `noCommit` the changes are staged
      and no commit is made; a pick which changes nothing is `Git.Error(NothingToCommit)`
      otherwise.
  entry #revert   applies the inverse of commits onto `HEAD`, as `cherryPick` does in reverse
  entry #rebase   replays the commits since `upstream` onto `onto`, stopping on a conflict
      Keeps its state in `rebase-merge/`, with a stopped pick already in `done`, as git
      arranges it. `Git.Rebase.Option`s for sign-off and the like are honoured;
      `--autosquash` is `Git.Error(Unsupported)`.
  entry #sequence   continues, skips or aborts the merge, cherry-pick, revert or rebase in progress
      Reads whichever state files are present to tell which operation it is;
      `Git.Error(NoOperationInProgress)` if none. Continuing an `am`-style rebase and
      skipping a merge are `Git.Error(Unsupported)`.
  entry #operation   the operation in progress, if any, with its conflicting paths
  entry #stash   saves the index and working changes as a stash entry and resets the tree
      Writes the three commits git writes (the index, the untracked files if asked, and the
      stash itself) under `refs/stash` with a reflog entry; `keepIndex` leaves the staged
      changes in place. A clean tree makes no entry and gives `Unset`; an index with unmerged
      entries is `Git.Error(Unmerged)`.
  entry #stashes   the stash entries, newest first, from the `refs/stash` reflog
  entry #applyStash   applies a stash entry to the tree, restoring the index too with `index`, and drops it with `drop`
      A missing entry is `Git.Error(NoSuchStash)`; conflicts stop it as a merge stops.
  entry #dropStash   removes a stash entry from the reflog
  entry #readNote   the note on an object under a notes ref, if there is one
  entry #listNotes   every note under a notes ref, each as the annotated object and the note blob
      Reads flat and fanned-out notes trees alike.
  entry #editNotes   adds, appends to, removes or copies a note, writing a new notes commit
      Takes the current tip of the notes ref as `base` (or `Unset` for the first note) and
      returns the new commit; the caller moves the ref. A note already present is
      `Git.Error(NoteExists)` unless the edit forces it, and copying from an object with none
      is `Git.Error(NoteNotFound)`.
  entry #configRead   the entries of one configuration file, or every entry in force
      `Git.Config.Source.Effective` gives the system, XDG, global, local and worktree files in
      git's order, with `include.path` files spliced in (to a depth of ten); any other source
      is read as one file.
  entry #configWrite   sets a key in a configuration file, or adds a further value with `add`
      Written under a `config.lock`, preserving comments and the rest of the file; a key in a
      new section gets the section header.
  entry #configUnset   removes a key, or with `all` every value of it, from a configuration file
  entry #remoteRefs   lists a remote's refs as `ls-remote` does, `HEAD` first and tags with their peeled targets
      Needs `Internet` evidence. The remote is a configured name, whose URL is
      `remote.<name>.url` (`remote.<name>.pushurl` for a push, when set), or a URL or path
      used as it is.
  entry #fetch   fetches from a remote, moves the local refs the refspecs name, and writes `FETCH_HEAD`
      Uses the remote's configured refspecs when none are given, negotiates with the peer's
      `download`, stores what arrives loose, cuts history to `depth` and records the boundary
      in `shallow`, adds `refs/tags/*:refs/tags/*` to the refspecs with `tags`, and with
      `prune` deletes the local refs matching a pattern destination whose remote counterpart
      has gone. Returns the refs it changed. Needs `Internet` evidence.
  entry #push   sends the objects a remote lacks and asks it to move its refs
      Each refspec becomes a `RefUpdate` whose expectation is set by the `Git.Push.Force`
      policy: a plain push expects the remote's current value and refuses a non-fast-forward
      move locally, `Always` expects nothing, and `Lease` expects the readings it carries. The
      outcomes name each ref as `Created`, `Updated`, `Deleted`, `UpToDate` or `Rejected` with
      the peer's reason; `setUpstream` configures the branch's remote and merge keys. Needs
      `Internet` evidence.
  entry #worktrees   the main worktree and every linked one, with its head, branch and lock state
      A linked worktree whose `gitdir` points at a directory which no longer exists is marked
      prunable.
  entry #addWorktree   checks a revision out into a new linked worktree, on a new branch if asked
      Registers it under `worktrees/<name>` with `gitdir`, `commondir` and its own `HEAD`,
      writes the `.git` file in the new tree, checks the files out and writes the index. A
      revision which is the name of an existing branch attaches `HEAD` to it unless `detach`
      is set; `newBranch` creates a branch there first (`Git.Error(Moved)` if it exists). A
      target which exists and is not empty is `Git.Error(Unexpected)`.
  entry #removeWorktree   deletes a linked worktree and its registry entry
      A locked worktree is `Git.Error(Locked)`, and one with changes is `Git.Error(Dirty)`
      without `force`.
  entry #lockWorktree   writes a `locked` file, with an optional reason, so the worktree is never pruned
      Locking a worktree already locked is `Git.Error(Locked)`.
  entry #unlockWorktree   removes a worktree's `locked` file
      A worktree which is not locked is `Git.Error(Unexpected)`.
  entry #moveWorktree   moves a linked worktree's directory and rewrites its registry entry
      A locked worktree is `Git.Error(Locked)`, and a destination which exists is
      `Git.Error(Unexpected)`.
  entry #pruneWorktrees   removes registry entries whose worktrees have gone, returning their paths
      A locked entry is kept.

entry .Transport   a way of reaching remote repositories at some kind of URL, for the native backend
  detail
      `Native` reaches a repository on the same filesystem by its path, with no help; any other
      URL — `https://…`, `ssh://…`, `host:path` — needs a `Transport` which `#accepts` it, taken
      from the list the backend was constructed with. The library ships three, each in its own
      component so that a program pays only for the one it uses: `SmartHttp()` (the `http`
      component) speaks git's smart HTTP protocol through whichever `Http.Backend` is in scope;
      `SshCommand()` (the `ssh` component) spawns the `ssh` command for each exchange; and
      `SshNative()` (the `sshNative` component) uses the pure-Scala SSH client.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp

      val backend: Git.Backend = Native(List(SmartHttp()))
      ```

      A transport is asked in list order, and the first to accept the URL connects to it: the
      `Peer` it gives back is the other end of the fetch or push, driven by the wire protocol
      (version 2 for `ls-refs` and `fetch`, the version 0 `receive-pack` exchange for pushing).
      A transport of one's own implements the two methods; a transport which connects over
      the wire protocol builds its `Peer` from a `Channel`, which carries the service
      advertisement and each request and response. `gitBackends.soundnessGit` has no
      transports at all.
  refer octogenarian.Native
  refer octogenarian.Peer
  refer octogenarian.Channel
  refer octogenarian.SshAddress
  entry #accepts   whether this transport can reach the URL
      Judged by the URL's shape alone: `SmartHttp` accepts `http://` and `https://`, and the
      SSH transports accept whatever `SshAddress.parse` understands.
  entry #connect   opens a connection to the repository at the URL, as a `Peer`
      Raises `Git.Error` when the connection cannot be made; the URL has already been accepted.
      The peer is a capability (`Peer^`), since it holds the connection.

entry .Peer   the other end of a fetch or push: lists refs, sends objects, receives objects and ref updates
  detail
      A `Peer` is what `Native` fetches from and pushes to. It lists its refs, sends the objects
      reachable from the ones asked for (stopping at those the asker already has), and receives
      objects together with ref updates, which it applies one by one and reports on. How that
      happens is the peer's concern: `Peer.Local` reads and writes a repository on this
      filesystem directly, and a transport's peer speaks the wire protocol over a `Channel`.
      A peer is obtained through `Transport#connect`, or by `Native` itself for a local path;
      a program ordinarily meets one only when writing a `Transport`.
  refer octogenarian.Transport
  refer octogenarian.RefUpdate
  refer octogenarian.Native
  entry .Local   a repository on this filesystem, reached by reading and writing its files directly
      `Peer.Local(location, identity)` takes the repository's `Git.Backend.Location` (as
      `Native#locate` finds it) and the identity to record in its reflogs. It is the peer
      `Native` uses for a path, or a `file://`, `./` or `../` URL. Its `refs` lists `HEAD`
      first and then every ref, an annotated tag followed by its peeled target as
      `<name>^{}`, as `ls-remote` does; its `upload` writes the objects loose and applies each
      `RefUpdate` by compare-and-swap, reporting `failed to update ref` for one which has
      moved, `failed to lock` for one locked by another process, and `branch is currently
      checked out` for the branch a non-bare repository has checked out, which is never
      updated under it.
  entry #refs   every ref with its hash, `HEAD` included, as `ls-remote` would list them
      An annotated tag is followed by its peeled target as `<name>^{}`.
  entry #symbolicHead   what `HEAD` is a symbolic ref to, if the peer says
      The ref `clone` checks out when no branch is named; `Unset` for a detached or unknown
      `HEAD`.
  entry #download   sends every object reachable from `wants` which `has` does not know to `store`
      `haves` are the commits the asker holds, for a peer which can only be told what the asker
      has rather than ask it. With a `depth`, commit history is cut that many commits deep and
      the commits at the boundary are returned, for the asker's `shallow` file; otherwise the
      result is empty. The `Git.Activity` is moved through the `Receiving` phase.
  entry #upload   receives the objects named, read through `read`, then applies the updates
      Returns each update's ref paired with the reason it was rejected, or `Unset` for one
      which was applied. The `Git.Activity` is moved through the `Writing` phase, measured by
      the object count.

entry .RefUpdate   a ref to change on a peer: what it is expected to hold now, and what it should hold
  detail
      One line of a push: `Native#push` builds a `RefUpdate` for each refspec and hands them to
      `Peer#upload`. The `expected` value is the compare-and-swap condition — the remote's
      current reading for a plain push, `Git.Backend.Expectation.Unconditional` for a forced
      one, and the lease's reading for `Git.Push.Force.Lease` — and `to` is `Unset` to delete
      the ref.
  refer octogenarian.Peer
  entry #ref   the full name of the ref, such as `refs/heads/main`
  entry #expected   what the ref must hold for the update to be applied
  entry #to   the hash to move the ref to, or `Unset` to delete it

entry .Channel   a connection to a remote's `git-upload-pack` or `git-receive-pack`, however it is carried
  detail
      The seam between a `Transport` and the wire protocol: a channel supplies the server's
      opening advertisement for a service and a way to send one request and read what comes
      back, in pkt-line framing, and the protocol client built over it does the rest. The
      three shipped transports each define one — `SmartHttp.Channel` over HTTP requests with
      the `Git-Protocol: version=2` header, `SshCommand.Channel` over one `ssh` process per
      exchange, and `SshNative.Channel` over one SSH connection with a channel per exchange —
      and a transport of one's own would too. The readers it returns are the library's own
      pkt-line readers, which are not part of the public API, so a `Channel` can only be
      written inside the library at present.
  refer octogenarian.Transport
  entry #advertise   the server's opening advertisement for a service, as a pkt-line reader
      The service is `git-upload-pack` or `git-receive-pack`; the protocol client reads the
      version line and capabilities from it.
  entry #exchange   sends one request to a service and returns a reader over the response
  entry #close   releases whatever the channel holds: a process, a connection

entry .SshAddress   the address of a repository reached over SSH, in either form git accepts
  detail
      Git takes an SSH remote as the URL `ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path`, or in the `scp`-like
      form `[user@]host:path`, which is an SSH address provided the colon comes before any
      slash (`localhost:/srv/repo.git`), and a local path otherwise (`./dir:x`). `SshAddress`
      is the parsed form of either, and the SSH transports accept exactly the URLs
      `.parse` understands.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      SshAddress.parse(t"ssh://git@example.com:2222/srv/repo.git")
      // SshAddress(t"git", t"example.com", 2222, t"/srv/repo.git")

      SshAddress.parse(t"git@example.com:propensive/soundness.git")
      // SshAddress(t"git", t"example.com", Unset, t"propensive/soundness.git")

      SshAddress.parse(t"https://example.com/repo.git")   // Unset
      ```

      In the URL form the path keeps its leading `/`; in the `scp` form it is whatever follows
      the colon, relative to the user's home on the server unless it begins with `/`.
  refer octogenarian.Transport
  entry .parse   reads an SSH address from text, or `Unset` if the text is not one
      Text beginning `ssh://` must have a `/` after the authority, and a port which is not a
      number makes the whole address `Unset`. Text with any other `://` is not an SSH address,
      and nor is text whose first `/` comes before its first `:`, or which has no colon.
  entry #user   the user to log in as, or `Unset` for the SSH client's default
  entry #host   the host name or address
  entry #port   the port, or `Unset` for the SSH client's default (22)
  entry #path   the repository's path on the server
